REV.page({"course":"english1","id":"all","title":"English 1: all weeks","summary":"The whole of English 1 on one page, for the last days before the exam. Every week in order, from the 44 sounds of English and the parts of speech to tenses, reading, listening, presentations and professional writing, with the lectures' rules, examples and common mistakes.","n":0,"lectures":[],"toc":[{"id":"the-course-at-a-glance","level":2,"title":"The course at a glance"},{"id":"week-1-learning-language-and-the-sounds-of-english","level":2,"title":"Week 1: Learning language and the sounds of English"},{"id":"how-we-learn-a-language","level":3,"title":"How we learn a language"},{"id":"letters-and-sounds","level":3,"title":"Letters and sounds"},{"id":"how-speech-sounds-are-made","level":3,"title":"How speech sounds are made"},{"id":"diphthongs-and-semi-vowels","level":3,"title":"Diphthongs and semi-vowels"},{"id":"consonant-clusters","level":3,"title":"Consonant clusters"},{"id":"week-2-parts-of-speech-and-articles","level":2,"title":"Week 2: Parts of speech and articles"},{"id":"the-parts-of-speech","level":3,"title":"The parts of speech"},{"id":"verbs-nouns-and-pronouns","level":3,"title":"Verbs, nouns and pronouns"},{"id":"adjectives-and-adverbs","level":3,"title":"Adjectives and adverbs"},{"id":"prepositions","level":3,"title":"Prepositions"},{"id":"conjunctions-and-interjections","level":3,"title":"Conjunctions and interjections"},{"id":"articles-a-an-the-and-the-zero-article","level":3,"title":"Articles: a, an, the and the zero article"},{"id":"week-3-vocabulary-modal-verbs-phrases-and-idioms","level":2,"title":"Week 3: Vocabulary, modal verbs, phrases and idioms"},{"id":"what-a-good-vocabulary-means","level":3,"title":"What a good vocabulary means"},{"id":"collocation","level":3,"title":"Collocation"},{"id":"inflection-and-derivation","level":3,"title":"Inflection and derivation"},{"id":"modal-verbs","level":3,"title":"Modal verbs"},{"id":"phrases-and-idioms","level":3,"title":"Phrases and idioms"},{"id":"week-4-spoken-english-tempo-pauses-talks-and-phone-calls","level":2,"title":"Week 4: Spoken English: tempo, pauses, talks and phone calls"},{"id":"what-matters-most","level":3,"title":"What matters most"},{"id":"speak-slowly","level":3,"title":"Speak slowly"},{"id":"phrasal-pause","level":3,"title":"Phrasal pause"},{"id":"something-to-say-a-short-talk","level":3,"title":"Something to say: a short talk"},{"id":"effective-presentations","level":3,"title":"Effective presentations"},{"id":"telephone-english","level":3,"title":"Telephone English"},{"id":"week-5-tenses-and-subject-verb-agreement","level":2,"title":"Week 5: Tenses and subject–verb agreement"},{"id":"the-twelve-forms","level":3,"title":"The twelve forms"},{"id":"subject-verb-agreement","level":3,"title":"Subject–verb agreement"},{"id":"week-6-reading-skills-comprehension-methods-and-tests","level":2,"title":"Week 6: Reading skills: comprehension, methods and tests"},{"id":"the-rules-for-comprehension","level":3,"title":"The rules for comprehension"},{"id":"critical-reading-facts-propaganda-and-reasoning","level":3,"title":"Critical reading: facts, propaganda and reasoning"},{"id":"skimming-and-scanning","level":3,"title":"Skimming and scanning"},{"id":"sq3r-and-pqrst","level":3,"title":"SQ3R and PQRST"},{"id":"short-texts-long-texts-and-the-critical-reader","level":3,"title":"Short texts, long texts and the critical reader"},{"id":"reading-tests","level":3,"title":"Reading tests"},{"id":"week-7-listening-active-and-passive-barriers-long-talks-and-","level":2,"title":"Week 7: Listening: active and passive, barriers, long talks and tests"},{"id":"active-and-passive-listening","level":3,"title":"Active and passive listening"},{"id":"barriers-to-listening","level":3,"title":"Barriers to listening"},{"id":"long-technical-talks","level":3,"title":"Long technical talks"},{"id":"predictive-listening","level":3,"title":"Predictive listening"},{"id":"listening-tests","level":3,"title":"Listening tests"},{"id":"week-8-syllables-word-stress-plurals-and-aspiration","level":2,"title":"Week 8: Syllables, word stress, plurals and aspiration"},{"id":"syllables","level":3,"title":"Syllables"},{"id":"word-stress","level":3,"title":"Word stress"},{"id":"plural-sounds","level":3,"title":"Plural sounds"},{"id":"aspiration","level":3,"title":"Aspiration"},{"id":"week-9-presentations-group-discussions-storytelling-and-spea","level":2,"title":"Week 9: Presentations, group discussions, storytelling and speaking tests"},{"id":"scripting-a-presentation","level":3,"title":"Scripting a presentation"},{"id":"slides-and-delivery","level":3,"title":"Slides and delivery"},{"id":"group-discussions","level":3,"title":"Group discussions"},{"id":"storytelling","level":3,"title":"Storytelling"},{"id":"speaking-tests","level":3,"title":"Speaking tests"},{"id":"week-10-common-errors-and-writing-basics","level":2,"title":"Week 10: Common errors and writing basics"},{"id":"nouns-and-verbs-that-break-the-pattern","level":3,"title":"Nouns and verbs that break the pattern"},{"id":"agreement-with-the-head","level":3,"title":"Agreement with the head"},{"id":"what-makes-writing-good","level":3,"title":"What makes writing good"},{"id":"bio-notes-photographs-and-public-writing","level":3,"title":"Bio-notes, photographs and public writing"},{"id":"week-11-notes-business-letters-instructions-data-reports-and","level":2,"title":"Week 11: Notes, business letters, instructions, data reports and essays"},{"id":"notes-and-business-letters","level":3,"title":"Notes and business letters"},{"id":"instructions-and-personal-details","level":3,"title":"Instructions and personal details"},{"id":"data-reports","level":3,"title":"Data reports"},{"id":"essays-and-simplicity","level":3,"title":"Essays and simplicity"},{"id":"week-12-professional-writing-paragraphs-formal-style-editing","level":2,"title":"Week 12: Professional writing: paragraphs, formal style, editing and proofreading"},{"id":"what-professional-writing-is","level":3,"title":"What professional writing is"},{"id":"building-a-paragraph","level":3,"title":"Building a paragraph"},{"id":"formal-style-and-objective-tone","level":3,"title":"Formal style and objective tone"},{"id":"editing-and-proofreading","level":3,"title":"Editing and proofreading"},{"id":"writing-for-an-exam","level":3,"title":"Writing for an exam"},{"id":"how-it-all-fits-together","level":2,"title":"How it all fits together"},{"id":"which-tense-when","level":3,"title":"Which tense when"},{"id":"which-method-when","level":3,"title":"Which method when"},{"id":"check-yourself","level":2,"title":"Check yourself"}],"html":"<h2 id=\"the-course-at-a-glance\">The course at a glance</h2>\n<p>The course has one goal, <strong>communicative confidence</strong>: <strong>accuracy</strong> (using the language correctly) leads to <strong>fluency</strong> (using it smoothly), and the two together give confidence (Lecture 1.1). It moves from small units to large ones. Weeks 1 to 3 build the materials: the sounds of English (Week 1), the parts of speech and the articles (Week 2), and words themselves, with modal verbs and idioms (Week 3). Then come the four skills that Lecture 1.2 asks you to practise every day: <strong>speaking</strong> (Weeks 4 and 9), <strong>reading</strong> (Week 6), <strong>listening</strong> (Week 7) and <strong>writing</strong> (Weeks 10 to 12). Two more grammar and sound weeks sit in between: tenses and subject–verb agreement (Week 5), and syllables, stress, plurals and aspiration (Week 8), which builds directly on the sounds of Week 1. The same few pieces of advice return again and again: have something to say, speak slowly, write simply, give facts, and revise.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Week</th>\n<th>Topics</th>\n<th>Key result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#/english1/w01\">1</a></td>\n<td>learning a language; letters and sounds; how sounds are made; diphthongs and semi-vowels; consonant clusters</td>\n<td>26 letters, but 44 sounds (20 vowels + 24 consonants)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#/english1/w02\">2</a></td>\n<td>the parts of speech; prepositions; conjunctions; articles</td>\n<td><em>a</em> or <em>an</em> goes by the next <strong>sound</strong>: <em>an hour, a European</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#/english1/w03\">3</a></td>\n<td>vocabulary; synonyms, homonyms, collocation; inflection and derivation; modal verbs; 60 idioms</td>\n<td>modal + bare verb: <em>can go</em>, never <em>can goes</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#/english1/w04\">4</a></td>\n<td>politeness, slow tempo and pauses; a short talk; presentations; telephone English</td>\n<td>pause after every phrase, clause and sentence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#/english1/w05\">5</a></td>\n<td>the 12 tense forms; subject–verb agreement</td>\n<td>the first auxiliary carries the tense; the verb agrees with the real subject</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#/english1/w06\">6</a></td>\n<td>comprehension; skimming and scanning; SQ3R and PQRST; short and long texts; critical reading; reading tests</td>\n<td>skim for the big picture, scan for one keyword</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#/english1/w07\">7</a></td>\n<td>active and passive listening; barriers; long talks; predictive listening; listening tests</td>\n<td>most barriers to listening lie inside us</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#/english1/w08\">8</a></td>\n<td>syllables; word stress; plural sounds; aspiration</td>\n<td><em>an OB-ject</em> but <em>to ob-JECT</em>; plural /s/ after p, t, k, else /z/</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#/english1/w09\">9</a></td>\n<td>script, slides and delivery; group discussions; storytelling; speaking tests</td>\n<td>PREP, past–present–future, problem–cause–solution, AIDA</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#/english1/w10\">10</a></td>\n<td>common errors; good writing; bio-notes; describing a photo; public writing</td>\n<td>the verb agrees with the <strong>head</strong> of the noun phrase</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#/english1/w11\">11</a></td>\n<td>notes; business letters; instructions; data reports; essays; simplicity</td>\n<td>facts, a fixed form, the most important thing first</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#/english1/w12\">12</a></td>\n<td>professional writing; paragraphs; formal style and tone; editing and proofreading; exam writing</td>\n<td>topic sentence + supporting sentences + concluding sentence</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2 id=\"week-1-learning-language-and-the-sounds-of-english\">Week 1: Learning language and the sounds of English</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Week 1 starts at the base of the language: how grown-ups learn a new language, and the sounds English is built from. The big lesson: English <strong>writing</strong> (letters) and English <strong>speech</strong> (sounds) are two separate systems, so you must learn the sounds apart from the spelling.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"how-we-learn-a-language\">How we learn a language</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Acquisition and learning</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Acquisition</strong>: a child gets its first language <strong>automatically</strong>, without effort, before about 10 years of age.</li>\n<li><strong>Learning</strong>: a grown-up (after about 15) gets a new language through <strong>conscious effort</strong>, helped by a <strong>specific and directed motivation</strong> (a clear reason to learn).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<ul>\n<li>A child is <strong>hardwired</strong> for language: <strong>input</strong> (what it hears: fuzzy and limited) → the <strong>mind</strong> (the Language Acquisition Device, LAD, and Universal Grammar, UG) → <strong>output</strong> (grammatical and infinite: it says sentences it has never heard).</li>\n<li>By 15 the first language has <strong>conditioned</strong> the <strong>vocal tract</strong> (the air passage from the lungs through the throat, mouth and nose). So do not try to copy an accent: “You have to sound like yourself” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DgKYu1pi3qg&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>). Aim to be clear.</li>\n<li>The grown-up’s method: <strong>observe the patterns</strong>, apply them, and let the subconscious work. There is nothing to memorise.</li>\n<li>The four skills are <strong>listening, speaking, reading, writing</strong>. Listen to English for <strong>30 minutes</strong> and read for <strong>30 minutes</strong> every day (a newspaper has “simple, active, affirmative sentences”). Speaking grows from these two; writing is a separate skill.</li>\n<li>The course works in the <strong>multilingual method</strong>: English as we use it in normal, natural situations. It builds confidence and <strong>cognitive flexibility</strong> (a flexible way of thinking).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w01/the-course-accuracy-fluency-confidence\">accuracy, fluency, confidence</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w01/how-we-learn-a-language\">how we learn a language</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"letters-and-sounds\">Letters and sounds</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Letters are not sounds</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>English writes with <strong>26 letters</strong> but speaks with <strong>44 sounds</strong>: <strong>20 vowels</strong> and <strong>24 consonants</strong>. There is “no direct one-to-one correspondence” between them (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xcc0U_U4xhg&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>). “A for apple” teaches the first <strong>letter</strong> of a word, not its first sound: <em>cat</em> begins with the letter c but the sound /k/. <strong>Phonetic transcription</strong> writes a word sound by sound in the <strong>IPA</strong> (International Phonetic Alphabet), with each sound between slashes. <em>Thing</em> has 5 letters but 3 sounds: /θɪŋ/.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The 44 sounds, grouped as on the slides of Lectures 1.3 and 2.5 (the mark ː means <strong>long</strong>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Group</th>\n<th>Sounds, with a key word for each</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>7 short vowels</td>\n<td>/ɪ/ pit, /e/ pet, /æ/ pat, /ɒ/ pot, /ʌ/ but, /ʊ/ book, /ə/ mother</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5 long vowels</td>\n<td>/iː/ bean, /ɜː/ burn, /ɑː/ barn, /ɔː/ born, /uː/ boon</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8 diphthongs</td>\n<td>/eɪ/ take, /aɪ/ buy, /ɔɪ/ boy, /ɪə/ fear, /eə/ care, /əʊ/ go, /ʊə/ poor, /aʊ/ cow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9 voiceless consonants</td>\n<td>/p/ pit, /t/ tab, /k/ cab, /tʃ/ chime, /f/ fan, /θ/ thin, /s/ sad, /ʃ/ shy, /h/ hot</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8 voiced consonants</td>\n<td>/b/ bit, /d/ dab, /g/ gab, /dʒ/ jam, /v/ van, /ð/ them, /z/ zoo, /ʒ/ treasure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7 other consonants</td>\n<td>/m/ man, /n/ not, /ŋ/ sing (nasals); /l/ lad, /r/ red (liquids); /w/ wed, /j/ yes (semi-vowels)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>So 7 + 5 + 8 = 20 vowels and 9 + 8 + 7 = 24 consonants: 44 sounds. Only 44 sounds make an infinite number of words.</p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Three steps of learning (Lecture 1.3)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Pay attention</strong> to differences and similarities; <strong>allow</strong> your subconscious to fix them; <strong>observe your own output</strong>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w01/26-letters-two-sets\">26 letters</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w01/a-for-apple\">A for apple</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w01/writing-a-sound\">writing a sound</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w01/the-44-sounds-of-english\">the 44 sounds</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"how-speech-sounds-are-made\">How speech sounds are made</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Vowel and consonant</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>All speech uses the air we breathe <strong>out</strong>. A <strong>vowel</strong> is made with minimal <strong>obstruction</strong> (nothing much in the way), so you can hold it: <em>aaa</em>. A <strong>consonant</strong> is made by blocking or narrowing the air at a <strong>place of articulation</strong>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Place</th>\n<th>Made with</th>\n<th>English sounds</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Bilabial</td>\n<td>both lips</td>\n<td>/p/, /b/, /m/</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Labiodental</td>\n<td>the lower lip and the upper teeth</td>\n<td>/f/, /v/</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dental</td>\n<td>the tongue tip at the teeth</td>\n<td>/θ/, /ð/</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alveolar</td>\n<td>the tongue tip at the ridge behind the upper teeth</td>\n<td>/t/, /d/, /n/, /s/, /z/, /l/, /r/</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Post-alveolar</td>\n<td>just behind that ridge</td>\n<td>/ʃ/, /ʒ/ (and /tʃ/, /dʒ/)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Palatal</td>\n<td>the tongue body at the hard palate (the roof of the mouth)</td>\n<td>/j/</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Velar</td>\n<td>the back of the tongue at the velum (the soft back of the roof)</td>\n<td>/k/, /g/, /ŋ/</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Glottal</td>\n<td>the glottis (the gap between the vocal folds)</td>\n<td>/h/</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Oral or nasal</strong>: with the uvula (the small flap at the back of the mouth) raised, all the air leaves through the mouth: an <strong>oral</strong> sound (<em>pa</em>). Lowered, some air also leaves through the nose: a <strong>nasal</strong> sound (<em>ma</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Manner</strong> (how the air passes): a <strong>stop</strong> blocks it fully, then lets it go (p, b, t, d, k, g); a <strong>fricative</strong> pushes it through a narrow gap with a hiss (f, v, θ, ð, s, z, ʃ, ʒ, h); a <strong>nasal</strong> sends it through the nose (m, n, ŋ); in /l/ it flows round the sides of the tongue.</li>\n<li><strong>Vowel length</strong> can change the word: <em>seat</em> (long /iː/) and <em>sit</em> (short /ɪ/). Vowels are also described by the part of the tongue that rises (<strong>front, central, back</strong>) and how high it rises (<strong>close, mid, open</strong>).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Aspiration and voicing</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Aspiration</strong>: a small extra <strong>puff of air</strong>. Hold your palm in front of your mouth: <em>kha</em> has it, <em>ka</em> does not. It is marked +asp or −asp.</li>\n<li><strong>Voicing</strong>: a <strong>vibration of the vocal cords</strong>. Put your palm on your throat: <em>ga</em> buzzes (<strong>voiced</strong>, +voice), <em>ka</em> does not (<strong>voiceless</strong>, −voice) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fC-UbX3MsGs&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>So k is −asp −voice, kh is +asp −voice, g is −asp +voice, and gh is +asp +voice. A place plus these two features names exactly one sound.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>English has <strong>no retroflex sounds</strong> (sounds made with the tongue tip curled back, common in Dravidian and Indo-Aryan languages). English t and d are <strong>alveolar</strong>: the tongue tip gently touches the ridge behind the upper teeth, without curling. English f is <strong>labiodental</strong> (the upper teeth touch the lower lip), not the bilabial ph of Hindi <em>phal</em>. Such differences give our English its Indianness: <strong>Indian English</strong> is a variety, like American or British English, and “there is nothing bad or offensive about it”.</p>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w01/vowels-and-consonants\">vowels and consonants</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w01/the-articulators\">the articulators</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w01/the-english-consonant-chart\">the English consonant chart</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w01/aspiration-and-voicing\">aspiration and voicing</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w01/english-has-no-retroflex-sounds\">no retroflex sounds</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"diphthongs-and-semi-vowels\">Diphthongs and semi-vowels</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Monophthong and diphthong</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>monophthong</strong> keeps one vowel quality from start to end (/ɪ/ in <em>sit</em>); English has <strong>12</strong>. A <strong>diphthong</strong> glides from one vowel to another inside one sound (/eɪ/ in <em>take</em>); English has <strong>8</strong>. 12 + 8 = 20 vowels. A diphthong is <strong>one</strong> sound: <em>take</em> /teɪk/ has three sounds, and <em>buy</em> /baɪ/ has two.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Semi-vowel</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>/w/ and /j/ (the y sound) are consonants made with no blockage, so they sound like vowels; but they are <strong>non-syllabic</strong>: they cannot make a <strong>syllable</strong> (a beat of a word, built around a vowel sound). They never end a word: <em>window</em> and <em>cow</em> end in the sounds /əʊ/ and /aʊ/, not /w/. In speech they <strong>join</strong> a vowel at the end of one word to a vowel at the start of the next: <strong>/w/</strong> after a <strong>rounded</strong> vowel (lips pushed into a circle), <strong>/j/</strong> otherwise.</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Phrase</th>\n<th>Said as</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Go away</em></td>\n<td>go-<strong>w</strong>-away</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Put your shoe on</em></td>\n<td>shoe-<strong>w</strong>-on</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>I am late.</em></td>\n<td>I-<strong>y</strong>-am late</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Say it.</em></td>\n<td>say-<strong>y</strong>-it</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w01/diphthongs\">diphthongs</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w01/semi-vowels\">semi-vowels</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w01/their-function-joining-two-vowels\">joining two vowels</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"consonant-clusters\">Consonant clusters</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Consonant cluster</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Consonant sounds together with no vowel between them: the built-in short “a” of the first consonant is dropped. <em>Glass</em> has the cluster gl at the start (pattern CCVC, where C = consonant and V = vowel); <em>sink</em> has nk at the end (CVCC). Every word needs a vowel, and most words alternate consonants and vowels.</p>\n</div></section>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The same-place rule</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Pj1iWVqBuyg&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>): the lecture says two sounds from the same place never form a cluster, unless one is a nasal. That is too broad: two non-nasals from the same place can cluster (<em>st</em> in <em>stress</em>, <em>sl</em> in <em>slow</em>, <em>tr</em> in <em>tree</em>). The safe form: two <strong>stops</strong> from the same place never cluster inside a simple word (<em>pb, td, kg</em>). A nasal and a stop are fine (<em>mb</em> in <em>number</em>).</li>\n<li>A cluster can come at the start, in the middle or at the end of a word, even twice: <em>cluster</em> (kl, st), <em>blast</em> (bl, st).</li>\n<li><strong>Three consonants at the start</strong> follow one template: <strong>s + p, t or k + r or l</strong>: <em>spring, stress, screw, splash, string, scrub</em>. The third sound can also be w or j, which the lecture leaves out: <em>square</em> and <em>squeeze</em> start with /skw/, <em>spew</em> with /spj/ and <em>skew</em> with /skj/. No English word begins with four.</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Can an English word begin with spl, sbr or ptr?</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>The template: first s, then a voiceless stop (p, t or k), then r or l (or w or j, as in <em>square</em> and <em>spew</em>).</li>\n<li><em>spl</em>: s, then p, then l → it fits, as in <em>splash</em>.</li>\n<li><em>sbr</em>: the second sound, b, is voiced, not p, t or k → no.</li>\n<li><em>ptr</em>: it does not begin with s → no.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> only <em>spl</em>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w01/words-as-patterns-of-c-and-v\">C and V patterns</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w01/manner-and-the-same-place-rule\">the same-place rule</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w01/clusters-of-three-consonants\">clusters of three</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Common mistakes in Week 1</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>Counting <strong>letters</strong> for <strong>sounds</strong>: the “five vowels” are five vowel letters; English has 20 vowel sounds.</li>\n<li>Reading symbols as letters: /j/ is the y of <em>yes</em> (the j of <em>jam</em> is /dʒ/); th is two sounds, /θ/ (<em>thin</em>) and /ð/ (<em>that</em>); /ŋ/ (<em>sing</em>), /tʃ/ and /dʒ/ are one sound each.</li>\n<li>Saying a diphthong as two sounds.</li>\n<li>Retroflex t and d in English, or the Hindi ph for f (<em>phather</em> for <em>father</em>).</li>\n<li>“<em>Window</em> ends in a semi-vowel”: the letter is w, but the sound is /əʊ/.</li>\n<li>Swapping voicing: /p/ is voiceless and /b/ is voiced (the video once says it the other way round; the slide is right).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>Full revision:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w01\">Week 1</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"week-2-parts-of-speech-and-articles\">Week 2: Parts of speech and articles</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Sounds make words, and words make a <strong>sentence</strong> (<em>Riyad plays cricket</em>). Inside a sentence each word does a job, and the name of that job is its <strong>part of speech</strong>. Decide it by the <strong>function</strong> in that sentence, not by the word alone: <em>sweet</em> is an adjective in <em>a very sweet girl</em> but a noun in <em>a fragrant sweet</em>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-parts-of-speech\">The parts of speech</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Part of speech</th>\n<th>Its job</th>\n<th>The lectures’ example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Verb</td>\n<td>shows an action or a state; carries the tense</td>\n<td>Riyad <strong>plays</strong> cricket; I <strong>am</strong> a teacher</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Noun</td>\n<td>names a person, place, thing, animal, feeling or idea</td>\n<td><strong>Arjun</strong> went to <strong>Greece</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pronoun</td>\n<td>replaces a noun</td>\n<td><strong>He</strong> enjoyed spending time with <strong>his</strong> mother</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Adjective</td>\n<td>describes a noun or a pronoun</td>\n<td>a <strong>big</strong> car; he was <strong>thin</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Adverb</td>\n<td>tells about a verb, an adjective or an adverb</td>\n<td>we walked <strong>slowly</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Preposition</td>\n<td>shows time, place or position, before a noun</td>\n<td><strong>on</strong> Monday</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Article</td>\n<td><em>a, an, the</em>, before a noun</td>\n<td><strong>a</strong> cat</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Conjunction</td>\n<td>joins words, phrases or clauses</td>\n<td>Jack <strong>and</strong> Jill</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Interjection</td>\n<td>shows a sudden emotion; stands apart</td>\n<td><strong>Ouch!</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Four more words you need. The <strong>subject</strong> is who or what the sentence is about, usually the doer (who? <em>Riyad</em>); the <strong>object</strong> receives the action (what? <em>cricket</em>); the <strong>predicate</strong> is everything except the subject. A <strong>phrase</strong> is a group of words without its own subject and verb (<em>in a beautiful garden</em>); a <strong>clause</strong> has its own subject and verb (<em>he is my friend</em>).</p>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w02/parts-of-speech\">parts of speech</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w02/subject-and-object\">subject and object</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"verbs-nouns-and-pronouns\">Verbs, nouns and pronouns</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Verb</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>Every sentence must have a verb, and <strong>only verbs carry tense</strong> (past, present, future).</li>\n<li>A <strong>main verb</strong> carries the action (<em>play, keep, get</em>). An <strong>auxiliary</strong> (helping) verb helps it: <em>be</em> (<em>am, is, are, was, were</em>), <em>do</em>, <em>have</em>, and <em>can, could, may, might, must, shall, should, will, would</em>. In <em>You must get them</em>, <em>must</em> helps <em>get</em>.</li>\n<li>In a clause, <strong>only one verb agrees</strong> with the subject: <em>he plays</em>, <em>they play</em>, <em>I am</em>, <em>you are</em> (<em>you</em> always takes the plural form). In <em>Your dogs like to go for a walk</em>, only <em>like</em> agrees; <em>to go for a walk</em> is a <strong>verbal noun</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RvcNFUfeqHI&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Noun</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>naming word</strong>. <strong>Countable</strong> nouns are <strong>proper</strong> (one particular person or place: <em>India, John</em>), <strong>class</strong> (the whole class: <em>cat, tiger</em>) or <strong>collective</strong> (a group taken as one: <em>jury, cabinet, police</em>). <strong>Uncountable</strong> nouns are <strong>abstract</strong> (an idea: <em>goodness, gravity</em>) or <strong>material</strong> (a substance: <em>gold, water</em>). Three signs of a noun: only a noun can be made plural; an article comes before it; a preposition comes before it.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>A <strong>pronoun</strong> replaces a noun: <em>Rahul went to Kerala with <strong>his</strong> mother. <strong>He</strong> really enjoyed spending time with <strong>his</strong> mother.</em> Subject pronouns: <em>I, you, he, she, it, we, they</em>. Object pronouns: <em>me, you, him, her, it, us, them</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w02/verbs\">verbs</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w02/nouns\">nouns</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w02/pronouns\">pronouns</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"adjectives-and-adverbs\">Adjectives and adverbs</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>An <strong>adjective</strong> describes a noun or a pronoun. Before the noun it is <strong>attributive</strong> (<em>an amazing car</em>; the lecture calls this “distributive”); after the verb it is <strong>predicative</strong> (<em>This car is big</em>; <em>He was thin</em>). Several adjectives before one noun go in the order <strong>opinion → size → colour → type</strong>: <em>an amazing big red sports car</em> (the slide puts commas between them; adjectives of different kinds in this order are usually written without commas).</li>\n<li>An <strong>adverb</strong> tells about a verb, an adjective or another adverb, and answers <strong>how, when, where, why, how often</strong>. A whole phrase can do this job.</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Take one sentence apart (the lecture’s sentence at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1xo6TWVjYbE&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>Yesterday evening, we walked somewhat slowly in a very beautiful garden.</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li>The verb is <em>walked</em>. How did we walk? <em>Slowly</em>: an adverb of the verb.</li>\n<li>How slowly? <em>Somewhat</em>: an adverb of the adverb <em>slowly</em>.</li>\n<li>What kind of garden? <em>Beautiful</em>: an adjective. How beautiful? <em>Very</em>: an adverb of an adjective.</li>\n<li>When? <em>Yesterday evening</em>. Where? <em>In a very beautiful garden</em>. Both phrases work as adverbs of <em>walked</em>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> adverbs <em>slowly, somewhat, very</em>; adverb phrases <em>yesterday evening, in a very beautiful garden</em>; one adjective, <em>beautiful</em>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w02/adjectives\">adjectives</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w02/adverbs\">adverbs</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"prepositions\">Prepositions</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Preposition</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A word that shows a relation of <strong>time, place or position</strong> between the noun after it and the rest of the sentence: <em>I will see you <strong>at</strong> the office <strong>on</strong> Monday.</em> A preposition is always followed by a noun (or noun phrase) or a pronoun, and the two make a <strong>prepositional phrase</strong>. Prepositions, pronouns and articles are a <strong>closed class</strong>: a short, fixed list of <strong>function words</strong>. Nouns, verbs and adjectives are an <strong>open class</strong>: new ones keep coming. Indian languages put such words after the noun, as <strong>postpositions</strong> (Hindi <em>ghar mein</em>, “house in”).</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Preposition</th>\n<th>Time</th>\n<th>Place</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>at</strong></td>\n<td>a point: <em>at 4.00 am, at noon</em></td>\n<td>a point: <em>at the library</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>on</strong></td>\n<td>a day or a date: <em>on Wednesday, on 25th June</em></td>\n<td>a surface: <em>on the terrace</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>in</strong></td>\n<td>a longer period: <em>in January, in 2017, in summer</em></td>\n<td>a space with a boundary: <em>in a room, in the sea</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>for</strong></td>\n<td>a duration: <em>for three days</em></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em>By</em> shows the agent or the means: <em>manufactured <strong>by</strong> machines</em>, <em>travels <strong>by</strong> bus</em>, <em>We were informed <strong>by</strong> the authorities.</em> <strong>Compound prepositions</strong> do the same job in two or more words: <em>according to, because of, in front of, in spite of, instead of</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w02/prepositions\">prepositions</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w02/time-place-direction-and-agent\">time, place, direction and agent</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w02/thirty-two-common-prepositions\">32 common prepositions</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"conjunctions-and-interjections\">Conjunctions and interjections</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>It joins</th>\n<th>Examples</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Coordinating</td>\n<td>equal parts, like with like</td>\n<td>the seven <strong>FANBOYS</strong>: <em>for, and, nor, but, or, yet, so</em>: <em>She tried <strong>and</strong> succeeded.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Subordinating</td>\n<td>a <strong>subordinate</strong> clause to a <strong>main</strong> clause</td>\n<td><em>although, because, if, since, unless, until, while, lest, whereas</em>; <em>as if, as soon as, even if</em>: <em><strong>As</strong> he is my friend, I will help him.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Correlative</td>\n<td>equal parts, with a pair of words</td>\n<td><em>both … and, either … or, neither … nor, not only … but also, no sooner … than, hardly … when</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>A <strong>main clause</strong> makes sense alone (<em>I will help him</em>); a <strong>subordinate clause</strong> does not (<em>as he is my friend …</em>). A sentence with both is a <strong>complex sentence</strong>. When <em>so</em> means “in order that”, formal writing says <em>so that</em>: <em>I am saving money so that I can buy a bicycle.</em></p>\n<p>An <strong>interjection</strong> (<em>wow, ouch, oops, argh, hi</em>) shows a sudden emotion. It stands apart from the rest of the sentence, carries no tense or number, and belongs to speech, not to formal writing.</p>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w02/conjunctions\">conjunctions</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w02/interjections\">interjections</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"articles-a-an-the-and-the-zero-article\">Articles: a, an, the and the zero article</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">A or an</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>A</em> and <em>an</em> are the <strong>indefinite</strong> articles: any one, not yet known. <em>The</em> is the <strong>definite</strong> article: a particular one that the listener can identify. Choose between <em>a</em> and <em>an</em> by the first <strong>sound</strong> of the next word: <strong>a</strong> before a consonant sound, <strong>an</strong> before a vowel sound (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KXWalyM03b8&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>). The <em>n</em> of <em>an</em> keeps two vowel sounds apart.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">A or an? (the lecture’s words)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><em>cat</em>: the first sound is /k/, a consonant → <strong>a</strong> cat.</li>\n<li><em>honest</em>: the h is silent, so the first sound is a vowel → <strong>an</strong> honest man (and <strong>an</strong> hour).</li>\n<li><em>MA</em>: we say “em”, which starts with a vowel sound → <strong>an</strong> MA.</li>\n<li><em>European</em>: the first sound is /j/, the y of <em>you</em>, a consonant → <strong>a</strong> European trip.</li>\n<li><em>one</em>: the first sound is /w/ → <strong>a</strong> one to one match.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> the sound decides, not the letter.</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>When</th>\n<th>Article</th>\n<th>The lectures’ examples</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>a first mention; any one</td>\n<td>a/an</td>\n<td><em>I saw a snake.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>an amount or a number</td>\n<td>a/an</td>\n<td><em>a lot of laddus; an hour; a couple of days</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a name that is not important to us</td>\n<td>a</td>\n<td><em>A Mr. Nikhil came to see you.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a name used figuratively</td>\n<td>a/an or the</td>\n<td><em>He wanted to be an Einstein. Kalidas is the Shakespeare of India.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a whole class</td>\n<td>a/an or the</td>\n<td><em>An owl can see in darkness. The crow is black.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>known already, or specified</td>\n<td>the</td>\n<td><em>The snake was a cobra. The book you gave me is missing.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the only one</td>\n<td>the</td>\n<td><em>The sun rises in the east.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a family</td>\n<td>the</td>\n<td><em>The Chettiars live next to my house.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>rivers, seas, mountain ranges, island groups, newspapers, classics</td>\n<td>the</td>\n<td><em>the Ganga, the Arabian Sea, the Himalayas, The Hindu, the Mahabharat</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a noun + <em>of</em>; a specified material</td>\n<td>the</td>\n<td><em>the front of my house; the water of the Ganga</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a superlative; <em>the more … the more</em></td>\n<td>the</td>\n<td><em>the brightest girl; The sooner you start, the better for you.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a daily-routine place</td>\n<td>none (the <strong>zero article</strong>)</td>\n<td><em>He is going home. She was admitted to hospital.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a material or abstract noun in general</td>\n<td>none</td>\n<td><em>five bars of gold; The small child needs care.</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Choosing an article in three steps</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>A daily-routine place (<em>home, church, hospital, bed</em>), or a material or abstract noun used in general? → <strong>no article</strong>.</li>\n<li>Can the listener tell exactly which one? → <strong>the</strong>.</li>\n<li>Otherwise, for a singular countable noun → <strong>a</strong> or <strong>an</strong>, by the first sound of the next word.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w02/articles-a-and-an\">a and an</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w02/five-uses-of-a-and-an\">five uses of a and an</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w02/the-definite-article\">the</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w02/the-zero-article\">the zero article</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Common mistakes in Week 2</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>(✗ marks a wrong sentence or form.)</p>\n<ul>\n<li>✗ <em>You is</em>: <em>you</em> always takes the plural form, <em>you are</em>.</li>\n<li>“Words ending in -ly are adverbs”: <em>well, fast, very, too, now</em> are adverbs too. Judge a word by its job.</li>\n<li>An adjective describes the noun after it: in <em>a majestic building with narrow corridors</em>, <em>narrow</em> goes with <em>corridors</em>.</li>\n<li>Giving a word one fixed class: <em>since</em> is a preposition in <em>since 2 pm</em> but a conjunction in <em>Since you are here, you can help me</em>.</li>\n<li>✗ <em>an European</em>, ✗ <em>a hour</em>: choose by the sound, not the letter.</li>\n<li>✗ <em>I go to the church every Sunday</em>: a routine place takes no article (<em>go to church, go home, admitted to hospital</em>).</li>\n<li><em>The</em> before a noun in apposition (a noun that renames the noun before it, usually between commas): write <em>Arundhati Roy, author of …</em>, not <em>the author of …</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>Full revision:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w02\">Week 2</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"week-3-vocabulary-modal-verbs-phrases-and-idioms\">Week 3: Vocabulary, modal verbs, phrases and idioms</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Vocabulary</strong> is all the words you know <strong>and</strong> all you know about each one: its meaning, spelling, pronunciation, part of speech, its forms, and the words it goes with (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A2eI-Ttmd7I&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>). A good vocabulary is not just a big number (Shakespeare knew fewer than 30,000 words); it is knowing words well and choosing the exact one. The week then teaches the <strong>modal verbs</strong> and sixty common <strong>phrases and idioms</strong>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"what-a-good-vocabulary-means\">What a good vocabulary means</h3>\n<p>Nobody knows exactly how many words they know: a child of five knows about <strong>5,000</strong>; a college graduate, <strong>30,000 to 50,000</strong>; people who read, write or teach for a living, more. A good vocabulary shows in five things (3.1):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sign</th>\n<th>The lecture’s example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1. Number and variety of words</td>\n<td><em>teacher, professor, instructor, preceptor</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2. Similar and opposite words</td>\n<td><em>road, avenue, lane</em>; <em>friend, enemy</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3. Which word goes with which</td>\n<td>you <em>drink</em> water but <em>eat</em> rice</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4. New words made from old</td>\n<td><em>person → personal, personify, personality</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5. Use in a sentence</td>\n<td><em>John reads books</em> (one person, so <em>-s</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Synonyms and antonyms</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Synonyms</strong> mean almost the same (<em>road, avenue, lane, street</em>); <strong>antonyms</strong> mean the opposite (<em>friend / enemy</em>). Knowing only <em>friends</em> and <em>enemies</em> is a <strong>functional vocabulary</strong> (enough to get by); knowing their synonyms and antonyms too is a <strong>good vocabulary</strong>. Synonyms are never exactly alike: <strong>fine</strong> slices (thin), a <strong>nice</strong> smell, <strong>excellent</strong> work (more than was asked), a <strong>marvellous</strong> child who can spell every word.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>A <strong>dictionary</strong> lists words in alphabetical order, with spelling, meaning, part of speech, usage and pronunciation. A <strong>thesaurus</strong> (such as <em>Roget’s Thesaurus</em>) groups words by meaning, so you can choose the accurate one. Keep both at hand.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Homophones and homonyms</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Homophones</strong> sound the same but differ in spelling and meaning: <em>right / write</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Homonyms</strong> share the spelling <strong>and</strong> the sound, but differ in meaning, often in part of speech too: <em>beat</em> (to hit) and <em>beat</em> (a policeman’s area); <em>board</em> (a group of managers / to climb onto a bus); <em>light</em> (not dark / not heavy).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>-phone</em> means sound (as in <em>telephone</em>), so a homo<strong>phone</strong> shares only the sound.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w03/what-is-vocabulary\">what is vocabulary</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w03/five-signs-of-a-good-vocabulary\">five signs</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w03/synonyms-and-antonyms\">synonyms and antonyms</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w03/dictionary-and-thesaurus\">dictionary and thesaurus</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w03/homophones-and-homonyms\">homophones and homonyms</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"collocation\">Collocation</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Collocation</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“For every word it is fixed which words it can follow, and which other words it can be followed by” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pHkc23_0ULI&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>). This fixed partnership between words is <strong>collocation</strong>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Countable nouns (<em>students, boys</em>)</th>\n<th>Uncountable nouns (<em>water, milk, sugar</em>)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>a large quantity</td>\n<td><strong>many</strong> students</td>\n<td><strong>much</strong> water</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a small quantity</td>\n<td><strong>few</strong> boys</td>\n<td><strong>little</strong> water</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Chennai has <strong>little</strong> water for so many people</em> (not <em>few water</em>: water cannot be counted).</li>\n<li><em>electric guitar, electric light</em>, but <em>electrical engineer</em>; <em>a cold day</em>, and also <em>a cold welcome</em>; clouds <em>float</em>, water <em>flows</em>.</li>\n<li><em>hard</em> or <em>soft</em>: a <em>hard copy</em> (on paper), a <em>soft copy</em> (a computer file), a <em>soft drink</em>, <em>hard work</em>, <em>hard luck</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w03/collocation\">collocation</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w03/adjectives-and-their-nouns\">adjectives and their nouns</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"inflection-and-derivation\">Inflection and derivation</h3>\n<p>In a sentence, words often change from their dictionary form. A <strong>marker</strong> is the small part that shows the change: <em>-s</em> (plural), <em>'s</em> (possessive), <em>-ing</em> (progressive), <em>-ed</em> (past), <em>-al</em>, <em>-tion</em>, <em>-ness</em>, <em>-ity</em>, <em>-ly</em>.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Inflection and derivation</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inflection</strong>: the form changes, but the <strong>category</strong> (part of speech) stays: <em>week → weeks</em> (still a noun), <em>come → came</em> (still a verb), <em>late → later</em> (the same part of speech, a new degree) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b8Wq_9P7uqI&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Derivation</strong>: the category changes: <em>politics → political</em> (noun → adjective), <em>acquire → acquisition</em> (verb → noun), <em>slow → slowly</em> (adjective → adverb) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b8Wq_9P7uqI&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Inflection or derivation? (words from the lecture’s passage)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Ask one question: is it still the same part of speech?</p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>days</em> = <em>day</em> + plural: still a noun → <strong>inflection</strong>.</li>\n<li><em>became</em> = <em>become</em> + past tense: still a verb → <strong>inflection</strong>.</li>\n<li><em>happiness</em> = <em>happy</em> (adjective) + <em>-ness</em>: now a noun → <strong>derivation</strong>.</li>\n<li><em>comers</em> = <em>come</em> (verb) + <em>-er</em> + <em>-s</em>: now a noun → <strong>derivation</strong>, although the <em>-s</em> alone would be inflection.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> inflection, inflection, derivation, derivation. The lecture’s slide lists 21 changed words from the passage, 14 inflections and 7 derivations; the passage has more.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w03/how-words-change-in-a-sentence\">how words change</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w03/two-kinds-of-change\">two kinds of change</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"modal-verbs\">Modal verbs</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Modal verb</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>modal</strong> (modal auxiliary) verb adds <strong>modality</strong> to the main verb: ability, permission, request, possibility, advice, obligation (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XFPZfdWCom4&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>). The modals are <em>can, could, may, might, shall, should, will, would, must, ought to, need</em>. After a modal, the main verb is a <strong>bare infinitive</strong> (the base form with no <em>to</em>): <em>I can run</em>, <em>He can swim</em>. Questions put the modal first (<em>Can you play the guitar?</em>); negatives add <em>not</em> (<em>cannot, must not</em>); speech uses short forms (<em>can’t, won’t, mustn’t</em>), but <em>may not</em> is never shortened.</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Modal</th>\n<th>Main uses, with the lectures’ sentences</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>can</td>\n<td>ability (<em>He can swim</em>); request and permission (<em>Can I speak to Mark, please?</em>); <em>can’t</em> = impossible (<em>It can’t be true</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>could</td>\n<td>past ability (<em>I could swim when I was six</em>); a polite request (<em>Could you tell me the time, please?</em>); a suggestion; a guess (<em>He could be French</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>may</td>\n<td>formal permission (<em>May I talk to you for a second?</em>); an uncertain future (<em>The train may be delayed</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>might</td>\n<td>an uncertain future (<em>It might rain today</em>); the past of <em>may</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>shall</td>\n<td>with <em>I</em> and <em>we</em>: suggestions (<em>Shall we go and see a film?</em>), offers (<em>Shall I do the washing up?</em>), the future</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>should</td>\n<td>advice (<em>You should put cream on your skin</em>); suggestions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>will, would</td>\n<td>the future; offers and promises (<em>I’ll do it for you</em>); <em>Would you like …?</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>must</td>\n<td>an obligation set by the speaker; strong advice; <em>mustn’t</em> = it is not allowed (<em>You mustn’t go swimming after lunch</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ought to</td>\n<td>like <em>should</em>, but prescriptive, like a rule (<em>You ought to obey the law</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>need</td>\n<td>mostly <em>needn’t</em> = it is not necessary (<em>We needn’t go immediately</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Groups of words that work like modals:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>have to</strong>: an obligation set by an organisation (<em>You have to have a visa to visit the USA</em>); also for the past and the future, which <em>must</em> cannot show (<em>We all had to give in our names</em>). <strong>Have got to</strong> is the everyday spoken form.</li>\n<li><strong>don’t have to</strong> = no obligation (<em>You don’t have to come</em>). But <strong>mustn’t</strong> = an obligation <strong>not</strong> to do it (<em>You mustn’t smoke in here</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>be able to</strong> (ability: <em>He is able to walk now</em>), <strong>be allowed to</strong> (permission: <em>Are we allowed to smoke?</em>), <strong>used to</strong> + verb (a past habit or state that is over: <em>I used to live in Brazil</em>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w03/modal-verbs\">modal verbs</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w03/the-modals-one-by-one\">the modals one by one</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w03/phrases-that-work-like-modals\">phrases that work like modals</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"phrases-and-idioms\">Phrases and idioms</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Phrase, euphemism, proverb, saying and idiom</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>phrase</strong> (in common usage): a group of words with a special meaning: <em>all rights reserved</em>.</li>\n<li>A <strong>euphemism</strong>: a polite word for something upsetting or embarrassing: <em>kick the bucket</em> = die.</li>\n<li>A <strong>proverb</strong>: a simple, traditional saying that states a truth: <em>An apple a day keeps the doctor away.</em></li>\n<li>A <strong>saying</strong>: any short expression that is memorable for its meaning or its style.</li>\n<li>An <strong>idiom</strong>: a phrase with a <strong>metaphorical</strong> (not literal) meaning, accepted in common use: <em>break a leg</em> = good luck.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>The sixty phrases of Lectures 3.5 and 3.6, grouped by theme, with the slides’ meanings:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Effort</strong>: <em>bite the bullet</em> (face something unpleasant bravely); <em>move heaven and earth</em> (make the greatest effort); <em>burn the candle at both ends</em> (work extremely hard); <em>take the bull by the horns</em> (face a difficulty directly); <em>keep the ball rolling</em> (continue the work); <em>step up your game</em> (start performing better); <em>get your act together</em> (organise yourself); <em>call it a day</em> (stop for now); <em>cut corners</em> (do it the fastest, cheapest way).</li>\n<li><strong>Wrong or wasted effort</strong>: <em>flog a dead horse</em> (waste effort on what is finished); <em>bark up the wrong tree</em> (be wrong about the way or the reason); <em>put the cart before the horse</em> (do things in the wrong order); <em>make a mountain out of a molehill</em> (exaggerate a small difficulty); <em>bite off more than you can chew</em> (take on too much); <em>look for a needle in a haystack</em> (look for what is almost impossible to find).</li>\n<li><strong>Luck, chances and hard choices</strong>: <em>break a leg</em> (good luck); <em>a dark horse</em> (an unexpected winner); <em>a blessing in disguise</em> (a good thing that looked bad at first); <em>every cloud has a silver lining</em> (every negative has a positive); <em>kill two birds with one stone</em> (two aims, one effort); <em>miss the boat</em> (miss the opportunity); <em>between the devil and the deep sea</em> (between two difficult situations).</li>\n<li><strong>Time</strong>: <em>once in a blue moon</em> (rarely); <em>at the eleventh hour</em> (at the last moment); <em>better late than never</em>; <em>a nine days’ wonder</em> (something short-lived); <em>put something in cold storage</em> (keep it pending).</li>\n<li><strong>Money</strong>: <em>money burns a hole in your pocket</em> (you spend it fast); <em>cut your coat according to your cloth</em> (live within your means); <em>cost an arm and a leg</em> (be very expensive); <em>a white elephant</em> (a costly but useless possession); <em>put all your eggs in one basket</em> (depend on a single plan).</li>\n<li><strong>People</strong>: <em>the apple of your eye</em> (a person you love very much); <em>crocodile tears</em> (false tears); <em>hold your tongue</em> (stay silent); <em>cry wolf</em> (call for help you do not need); <em>sell someone out</em> (betray them); <em>sit on the fence</em> (stay undecided); <em>beat around the bush</em> (avoid saying what you mean); <em>break the ice</em> (help strangers relax); <em>be in the driving seat</em> (be in control); <em>the ball is in your court</em> (it is your turn to act); <em>it takes two to tango</em> (both share the responsibility); <em>a taste of your own medicine</em> (being treated as you treat others); <em>take the law into your own hands</em> (punish by your own idea of justice); <em>pull yourself together</em> (calm down and act normally); <em>get something out of your system</em> (get rid of a feeling by expressing it); <em>add insult to injury</em> (make a bad situation worse); <em>get out of hand</em> (become hard to control).</li>\n<li><strong>Knowing and saying</strong>: <em>a piece of cake</em> (very easy); <em>hear something on the grapevine</em> (hear a rumour); <em>actions speak louder than words</em>; <em>be all Greek to someone</em> (not be understood at all).</li>\n<li><strong>Out of</strong>: <em>out of my league</em> (at a higher level than me), <em>out of order</em> (not working), <em>out of place</em> (different from the surroundings), <em>out of character</em> (unlike one’s usual behaviour), <em>out of shape</em> (not fit), <em>out of the loop</em> (uninformed), <em>out of touch</em> (not in contact).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w03/phrases-and-idioms\">phrases and idioms</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w03/common-phrases-from-lecture-3-5\">phrases 1 to 30</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w03/common-phrases-from-lecture-3-6\">phrases 31 to 60</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w03/phrases-that-look-alike\">phrases that look alike</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Common mistakes in Week 3</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>✗ <em>much students</em>, ✗ <em>many water</em>, ✗ <em>few water</em>: use <em>many</em> and <em>few</em> with countable nouns, <em>much</em> and <em>little</em> with uncountable ones.</li>\n<li>✗ <em>electric engineer</em>: the collocation is <em>electrical engineer</em>.</li>\n<li>Mixing up homophone (same sound) and homonym (same spelling and sound).</li>\n<li>✗ <em>He can swims</em>, ✗ <em>He can to swim</em>, ✗ <em>You should to go</em>: modal + bare verb. <em>Ought to</em> is one unit: <em>You ought to go</em>.</li>\n<li>Mixing up <em>mustn’t</em> (you are not allowed) and <em>don’t have to</em> (you need not).</li>\n<li><em>Used to</em> + verb (a past habit: <em>I used to wake up at 5</em>) is not <em>be used to</em> + <em>-ing</em> (accustomed: <em>I am used to waking up at 5</em>).</li>\n<li>Changing a word of an idiom: say <em>bite the bullet</em>, <em>the apple of my eye</em>, <em>a nine days’ wonder</em>, <em>beat around the bush</em> exactly.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>Full revision:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w03\">Week 3</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"week-4-spoken-english-tempo-pauses-talks-and-phone-calls\">Week 4: Spoken English: tempo, pauses, talks and phone calls</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>English is the world’s language, spoken in every accent, so aim at <strong>global standard English</strong>: the English “which can take us to everyone”. Lecture 4.1 puts the priorities in order: first <strong>have something to say</strong>, then be <strong>polite</strong>, then speak <strong>slowly</strong>. Then come word stress and vowel length. Good speaking grows from good listening.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"what-matters-most\">What matters most</h3>\n<p>The slide “In learning to speak, remember the following” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mD9iGMD73g&amp;t=444s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:24</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Have something to say</strong>: something interesting, relevant and helpful. Jesus Christ, Prophet Muhammad and Gautama Buddha are known all over the world for what they said, though many of us do not know which language they spoke.</li>\n<li><strong>Politeness</strong> is more important than even pronunciation and grammar: listeners forgive a slip of grammar, “but they won’t pardon you for being rude”.</li>\n<li>A <strong>slow tempo</strong> (the speed of your speech) is a marker of politeness.</li>\n<li>Different words are stressed differently: <strong>LON</strong>-don, ber-<strong>LIN</strong>, ja-<strong>PAN</strong>; <strong>U</strong>-nion but u-<strong>NI</strong>-ted. The <strong>stressed syllable</strong> is the beat said with more force.</li>\n<li>English vowels are very long (<em>car</em>, <em>boat</em>) or very short (the <em>a-</em> of <em>about</em>, the <em>be-</em> of <em>before</em>).</li>\n<li>Good spoken English is a product of good <strong>listening</strong> habits: notice the <strong>manner</strong> of speech, not only the matter.</li>\n<li>Books and a dictionary: keep an advanced learner’s dictionary on your phone, and drill the words you say wrongly for 10 minutes in the morning and 10 in the evening.</li>\n</ol>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Politeness in English</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>English has one <em>you</em> for everybody, so it shows respect in other ways: polite words (<em>please, thanks, excuse me, sorry, pardon me</em>), gentle intonation, a slow tempo, and every request put as a <strong>question</strong>. Not ✗ <em>Tell me time, please</em> but <em>Will you tell me the time, please?</em></p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w04/english-for-the-world-what-matters-most\">what matters most</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w04/politeness\">politeness</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w04/word-stress-varies\">word stress</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w04/long-and-short-vowels\">long and short vowels</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"speak-slowly\">Speak slowly</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">English is a slow-tempo language</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>English has many long vowels, and its speakers pause after groups of words, after clauses and after sentences, not only at the end. Slow speech is easy to follow, it is <strong>polite</strong>, and it gives the speaker time to think. <strong>Rapid speech</strong> distorts sounds: they merge, drop out or change.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Pause is the punctuation of speech</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“A woman without her man is nothing” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=d2iAbGoxazg&amp;t=402s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:42</a>) can be said in two ways:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>A woman, without her man, is nothing</em>: the woman is nothing without the man.</li>\n<li><em>A woman, without her, man is nothing</em>: the man is nothing without the woman.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The same words give opposite meanings; only the pauses tell them apart.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>How fast is too fast (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=d2iAbGoxazg&amp;t=1530s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:30</a>)? More than <strong>3 or 4 syllables a second</strong>, or more than <strong>100 words a minute</strong>, is rapid. Pilots talking to air traffic control must stay under 100 words a minute. The self-test: count the words of a printed paragraph, record yourself reading it, and compare the words with the time. Your tempo is the same in every language, so practise slow speech in your mother tongue too, and switch to it for formal talk; it takes about 2 to 3 weeks.</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Are you a rapid speaker? (my numbers)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>You read a 200-word paragraph in 1 minute 20 seconds.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Time in seconds: 60 + 20 = 80.</li>\n<li>Words per second: 200 ÷ 80 = 2.5.</li>\n<li>Words per minute: 2.5 × 60 = 150.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 150 is more than 100, so you are a rapid speaker. Slow down and pause after each group of words.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w04/speak-slowly\">speak slowly</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w04/slow-speech-is-polite-speech\">slow speech is polite speech</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w04/how-to-learn-to-speak-slowly\">how to learn to speak slowly</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"phrasal-pause\">Phrasal pause</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Phrase and phrasal pause</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>phrase</strong> is one or more words that behave like one unit: <em>a committee of experts</em> works like a single noun. Speakers of standard English pause after every <strong>phrase</strong>, every <strong>clause</strong> and every <strong>sentence</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tGch4v-bKsU&amp;t=669s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:09</a>). The slides mark a short pause <strong>#</strong> and a long pause <strong>##</strong>; a phrase pause is shorter than a clause pause, which is shorter than a sentence pause. The pause is “not long, but it is noticeable”.</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Pause after</th>\n<th>The lectures’ example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>a greeting, an identity</td>\n<td><em>Good evening # this is All India Radio #</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a word of address (in mid-sentence, before it too)</td>\n<td><em>May I borrow this book # professor #</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>every 2 or 3 digits</td>\n<td><em>94 # 43 # 02 # 84 # 34</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>each level of an address</td>\n<td><em>D4/12 # 2nd Link Road # IIT Campus # Chennai 600 # 036</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a discourse marker</td>\n<td><em>so # invest in your health ##</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>each item in a series</td>\n<td><em>corns # milk # eggs # bread #</em> (the slide’s words; standard English says <em>some corn</em> or <em>cornflakes</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>every noun, verb, adjective and adverb phrase</td>\n<td><em>The black rose # is the loveliest of flowers ##</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>A <strong>word of address</strong> is the name or title you use when you speak <em>to</em> someone (<em>professor, doctor, Liz</em>). A <strong>discourse marker</strong> signals a turn in the talk: a conclusion (<em>so, therefore</em>), a contrast (<em>but</em>) or emphasis (<em>of course</em>).</p>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w04/phrasal-pause\">phrasal pause</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w04/where-to-pause\">where to pause</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"something-to-say-a-short-talk\">Something to say: a short talk</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">A two-minute talk on a civic problem</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>civic</strong> problem is a problem of a town and its people. Say <strong>where</strong>, <strong>what</strong>, <strong>how much</strong> (<strong>quantification</strong>: how big the problem is, in numbers) and <strong>its effects</strong>. In two minutes you must mention at least the name of the problem, the name of the place, its extent, and its harmful impact (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfbkqslFsTA&amp;t=753s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:33</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Content</strong> makes great speakers (Gandhi: “An eye for an eye only ends up making the whole world blind”): it matters more than pronunciation, grammar or vocabulary.</li>\n<li><strong>Quantify</strong>: not <em>It’s noisy</em> but <em>Even at five in the morning the locality has 54 decibels of noise</em>. Find data on the Net, in libraries and archives, in local folklore and songs, and from elders.</li>\n<li>Make it <strong>interesting</strong> (good news, a good word of praise, entertainment: a joke is “like salt in your food”) and <strong>relevant</strong> (no “flying to fish”). Show a photograph or a map. Check your words in a dictionary and a thesaurus: <em>waterlogged road</em> is better than “water logging on the road”.</li>\n<li>Rehearse with a timer and <strong>never exceed your time</strong>: “cut your coat according to your cloth”.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w04/do-you-have-something-to-say\">something to say</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w04/quantify-and-find-the-data\">quantify and find the data</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w04/make-it-interesting-and-relevant\">interesting and relevant</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"effective-presentations\">Effective presentations</h3>\n<p>“All conversations are presentations. All presentations are stage performances.” So:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>be <strong>well turned out</strong> (washed, groomed and dressed) and dressed for the occasion: not over-dressed, not under-dressed;</li>\n<li>relax, smile and greet your audience: “people see more than they listen” (in the finger experiment, most people touch their <strong>cheek</strong>, as the speaker does, not their <strong>chin</strong>, as he says);</li>\n<li>be <strong>relaxed but alert</strong>, and use your hands with meaning, not in “mindless flailing”;</li>\n<li>keep <strong>eye contact</strong>, panning slowly across the room: “You lose eye contact, and you lose your audience”;</li>\n<li><strong>modulate</strong> your voice (change its loudness, speed and tone to suit what you say);</li>\n<li>do not overload slides: a few bullets, a few words a line, charts for numbers, colours that contrast (jet black on turmeric yellow, like railway station boards);</li>\n<li>“Effective presentation requires an intelligent combination of <strong>creativity, language and data</strong>.”</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w04/effective-presentations\">effective presentations</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w04/posture-hands-and-eyes\">posture, hands and eyes</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w04/slides\">slides</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"telephone-english\">Telephone English</h3>\n<p>You <strong>make</strong> a call (you dial) or <strong>take</strong> a call (you pick up the phone). A call to a boss, a colleague or a company is <strong>formal</strong>; a call to family and friends is <strong>informal</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>What to say</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Start (formal)</td>\n<td><em>Hello. This is Megha from Airvoice Services.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Start (informal)</td>\n<td><em>How is it going? What’s up?</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ask for someone</td>\n<td><em>May I speak to Padmini, please?</em> (formal) / <em>Is Padmini there?</em> (informal)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Say why you call</td>\n<td><em>I’m calling to ask about …</em> / <em>Could you tell me …?</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Answer a call</td>\n<td><em>Good afternoon. Airvoice Services. Megha speaking. How may I help you?</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ask who it is</td>\n<td><em>May I ask who’s calling?</em> / <em>Who am I speaking to, please?</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Make someone wait</td>\n<td><em>Could you hold on?</em> / <em>Would you mind holding, please?</em> / <em>Sorry to keep you waiting.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A message</td>\n<td><em>Could you take a message for him?</em> / <em>Please ask her to call me back.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>You are busy</td>\n<td><em>I am tied up at the moment. Can I call you back in an hour’s time?</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>End</td>\n<td><em>No problem. I will try again later.</em> (formal) / <em>Take care. Let us be in touch.</em> (informal)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Phone words: <em>put someone through</em> (connect them), <em>buzz</em> or <em>ping</em> (call, informally), <em>rope in</em> or <em>patch in</em> (add someone to a call), <em>your voice is breaking</em>, <em>the line is engaged</em> (busy), <em>the line is unobtainable</em> (no connection), <em>hang up</em> (end the call). To a caller who asks for someone not at your number, say <em>Sorry, I think you have got the wrong number</em>, not just “Wrong number”.</p>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w04/telephone-english\">telephone English</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w04/phone-problems\">phone problems</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w04/ending-a-call\">ending a call</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Common mistakes in Week 4</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>Bare commands (<em>Close the window</em>): give requests as questions, with <em>please</em>.</li>\n<li>Speaking fast to sound fluent: fast speech is unclear, and in English it sounds impolite.</li>\n<li>Pausing only at full stops, or pausing in the middle of a unit.</li>\n<li>Saying a phone number in one breath: group the digits in twos or threes.</li>\n<li>A talk with no numbers in it: quantify the problem.</li>\n<li>Going over your time, even by a second.</li>\n<li>Reading out every word of a crowded slide.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>Full revision:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w04\">Week 4</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"week-5-tenses-and-subject-verb-agreement\">Week 5: Tenses and subject–verb agreement</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Tense</strong> is the verb form that places an action in <strong>time</strong>: past, present or future. <strong>Aspect</strong> adds how the action sits in that time: <strong>simple</strong> (plain, absolute time), <strong>perfect</strong> (one time embedded in, that is before, another: <strong>relational</strong> time), <strong>progressive</strong> (the action is in progress), <strong>perfect progressive</strong> (an embedded action in progress). Three tenses × four aspects = <strong>twelve forms</strong>. Then <strong>agreement</strong>: the verb must carry the person and number of its subject.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Words for verb forms: <strong>V1</strong> is the base form (<em>return, eat, go</em>); <strong>V2</strong> is the simple past (<em>returned, ate, went</em>); <strong>V3</strong> is the <strong>past participle</strong> (<em>returned, eaten, gone</em>); the <strong>-ing form</strong> is <em>eating</em>. <strong>Regular</strong> verbs add <em>-ed</em>; <strong>irregular</strong> verbs change inside (<em>drive, drove, driven</em>). An <strong>auxiliary</strong> helps the main verb: <em>be, do, have</em>, and the modal <em>will</em>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-twelve-forms\">The twelve forms</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Form</th>\n<th>Structure</th>\n<th>The lectures’ example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Simple past</td>\n<td>V2; <em>did</em> + V1 in negatives and questions</td>\n<td><em>She returned from the conference yesterday.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Past perfect</td>\n<td><em>had</em> + V3</td>\n<td><em>She had returned from the conference before the semester began.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Past progressive</td>\n<td><em>was / were</em> + V-<em>ing</em></td>\n<td><em>She was walking back to her lab.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Past perfect progressive</td>\n<td><em>had been</em> + V-<em>ing</em></td>\n<td><em>She had been writing a book on climate change before she published her recent work.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Simple present</td>\n<td>V1 or V1 + <em>-s</em>; <em>do / does</em> + V1</td>\n<td><em>He reads the newspaper in the morning.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Present perfect</td>\n<td><em>has / have</em> + V3</td>\n<td><em>The train has finally arrived.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Present progressive</td>\n<td><em>am / is / are</em> + V-<em>ing</em></td>\n<td><em>She is eating a mango.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Present perfect progressive</td>\n<td><em>has / have been</em> + V-<em>ing</em></td>\n<td><em>The baby has been crying for long.</em> (Standard: <em>for a long time</em>.)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Simple future</td>\n<td><em>will</em> + V1</td>\n<td><em>It will rain in the month of June.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Future perfect</td>\n<td><em>will have</em> + V3</td>\n<td><em>She will have completed her work by then.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Future progressive</td>\n<td><em>will be</em> + V-<em>ing</em></td>\n<td><em>She will be taking the train to work from now on.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Future perfect progressive</td>\n<td><em>will have been</em> + V-<em>ing</em></td>\n<td><em>I will have been living in Chennai for over ten years.</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Who carries the tense?</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Only <strong>one</strong> part of the verb group carries the tense: the V2 in the simple past affirmative (<em>returned, drove</em>), or else the <strong>first auxiliary</strong>: <em>did, had, was, were</em> (past); <em>do, does, has, have, am, is, are</em> (present); <em>will</em> (future, a modal that suggests prediction). The V3 and the <em>-ing</em> form never carry tense. The simple present affirmative has <strong>no visible tense marker</strong>: the <em>-s</em> of <em>she drinks</em> is an <strong>agreement</strong> marker, and it disappears in <em>they drink</em>, which is still present (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9lzN9jZiWiw&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Name the form (the lecture’s sentence)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>She had been knitting the quilt for the baby before the family arrived for Christmas.</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Read the verb group from the left: <em>had</em> carries the tense → <strong>past</strong>.</li>\n<li><em>had</em> + <em>been</em> (the V3 of <em>be</em>) → <strong>perfect</strong>.</li>\n<li><em>knitting</em> = <em>knit</em> + <em>-ing</em> → <strong>progressive</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> past perfect progressive. The knitting (event 1) was in progress before the family arrived (event 2).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>What each form means:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Simple past</strong>: past and finished; it also narrates stories (<em>One evening after dark a young man prowled among these crumbling red mansions</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Past perfect</strong>: the earlier of two past events, “past before past” (<em>They had reached home before the parcel arrived</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Simple present</strong>: a present state (<em>I want a pencil and a pen</em>), a habit (<em>He reads the newspaper in the morning</em>), an action as you speak (<em>Here comes my friend, Shikha</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Present perfect</strong>: a past action that matters now (<em>I have learned to drive</em>). <strong>Time adverbials</strong> (words that say when or for how long) go with it: <em>for</em> + a duration (<em>for a long time</em>), <em>since</em> + a point of time (<em>since December 2021</em>), <em>just, already, yet</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Present progressive</strong>: in progress now, for a limited time; commoner in speech than in academic writing.</li>\n<li><strong>Present perfect progressive</strong>: began in the past and goes on (<em>The baby has been crying for long</em>; in a positive sentence, standard English says <em>for a long time</em>), just stopped (<em>It has been raining all day and now it is all clear</em>), or a new habit (<em>Recently, I have been practising yoga</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Future</strong>: <em>will</em> + V1, often with a time adverbial (<em>tomorrow, next March, in the morning</em>). The future progressive gives a timetabled future (<em>They will be having lunch around 12:00 p.m.</em>), a routine, or a habit. The future perfect progressive is “a bit contentious”: not every verb fits it.</li>\n<li>Verbs of the mind (<em>enjoy, appreciate, love, understand</em>) usually stay simple: <em>I understand the lesson</em>, not <em>I am understanding</em>. This holds best for state verbs (<em>understand, know</em>); <em>I am enjoying the party</em> is correct.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w05/tense-and-aspect\">tense and aspect</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w05/the-past-tense\">the past</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w05/the-present-tense\">the present</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w05/the-future-tense\">the future</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w05/the-twelve-forms-together\">the twelve forms</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"subject-verb-agreement\">Subject–verb agreement</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Agreement</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The subject’s <strong>person</strong> (first person: the speaker, <em>I, we</em>; second: the listener, <em>you</em>; third: everyone and everything else) and <strong>number</strong> (singular: one; plural: more than one) must show up on the verb. <strong>Gender</strong> plays no part in English agreement. <em>Raju likes pizza</em> (third person singular → <em>-s</em>); <em>Raju and Ramu like pizza</em> (plural → no ending, a “zero” marker). <em>I</em> and <em>you</em> follow the plural pattern (<em>I go, you go, I don’t</em>), and <em>am</em> belongs to <em>I</em> alone.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The real work is to <strong>find the subject</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=1965s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:45</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Rule</th>\n<th>The lectures’ sentences</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>and</em> makes a plural</td>\n<td><em>Annie and her brothers <strong>are</strong> at school.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>singulars joined by <em>or / nor</em> stay singular</td>\n<td><em>The dog or the cat <strong>is</strong> outside.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a singular and a plural joined by <em>or / nor</em>: the part nearer the verb decides</td>\n<td><em>Either my shoes or your coat <strong>is</strong> always on the floor.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a phrase between the subject and the verb changes nothing</td>\n<td><em>One of the boxes <strong>is</strong> open. The team captain, as well as his players, <strong>is</strong> anxious.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>each, either, neither, everyone, nobody, something</em> are singular</td>\n<td><em>Everybody <strong>knows</strong> Mr. Ram. Either <strong>is</strong> correct.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>mathematics, politics, civics, measles, news</em> are singular</td>\n<td><em>The news <strong>is</strong> on at six.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>an amount of time or money is singular</td>\n<td><em>Five years <strong>is</strong> not a long time in history. Five dollars <strong>is</strong> a lot of money.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>collective nouns (<em>team, committee, family, class</em>) are singular when the group acts as one unit (when the members act separately, British English allows a plural: <em>The jury were divided</em>)</td>\n<td><em>The team <strong>runs</strong> during practice. The family <strong>has</strong> a long history.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>police, glasses, jeans, scissors, trousers</em> are plural</td>\n<td><em>Police <strong>are</strong> vigilant during festivals. These scissors <strong>are</strong> dull.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>sheep</em> and <em>deer</em> do not change, so the verb shows the number</td>\n<td><em>The sheep <strong>is</strong> a harmless creature. Deer <strong>are</strong> free on IIT Madras campus.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>there</em> is an empty subject: the noun after the verb decides</td>\n<td><em>There <strong>are</strong> several monkeys on the tree.</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">One of the boxes (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ALjHStRxE7M&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>One of the boxes ___ open.</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Find the subject. It is not <em>the boxes</em>: it is <strong>one</strong> of them. <em>Of the boxes</em> only says which group the one comes from.</li>\n<li><em>One</em> is singular.</li>\n<li>So the verb is singular.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>One of the boxes <strong>is</strong> open</em>, not <em>are</em>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w05/subject-verb-agreement\">subject–verb agreement</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w05/nouns-that-need-a-second-look\">nouns that need a second look</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w05/agreement-practice\">agreement practice</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Common mistakes in Week 5</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>Two tense markers: ✗ <em>She did not finished</em>, ✗ <em>What did he ate?</em> After <em>did</em>, use the bare verb.</li>\n<li>V2 after <em>had, has, have</em>: ✗ <em>had gave</em>, ✗ <em>have ate</em> → <em>had given</em>, <em>have eaten</em>.</li>\n<li>✗ <em>She will visits</em>, ✗ <em>She will has completed</em>: after <em>will</em>, the bare verb, and <em>have</em> for every subject.</li>\n<li>Calling the <em>-s</em> of <em>drinks</em> a tense marker: it marks agreement.</li>\n<li>Agreeing with the nearest noun: ✗ <em>One of the boxes are open</em>.</li>\n<li>✗ <em>I doesn’t know</em>, ✗ <em>You doesn’t</em>: <em>I</em> and <em>you</em> take <em>don’t</em>.</li>\n<li>✗ <em>Police is</em> (police is always plural) and ✗ <em>The team are</em> (in this course, collective nouns acting as one unit are singular).</li>\n<li>✗ <em>I am understanding</em>: state verbs of the mind (<em>understand, know</em>) stay simple.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>Full revision:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w05\">Week 5</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"week-6-reading-skills-comprehension-methods-and-tests\">Week 6: Reading skills: comprehension, methods and tests</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Reading builds your <strong>vocabulary</strong> and your <strong>grammar</strong>, and these two make your writing and speaking better (6.1). This week gives rules for understanding a text, two fast ways to read (skimming and scanning), two methods for studying a chapter (SQ3R and PQRST), ways to read short and long texts, <strong>critical reading</strong> (judging what you read), and a plan for timed reading tests.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-rules-for-comprehension\">The rules for comprehension</h3>\n<p><strong>Comprehension</strong> means understanding; in an exam it is a <strong>passage</strong> with questions on it. The eight rules (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zRJUr8r79IQ&amp;t=65s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:05</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Read out loud</strong>, at times: it is slow reading, with <strong>inflection</strong> (the voice rising and falling) and pauses at the punctuation, and you also hear what you read.</li>\n<li><strong>Read with a pen</strong>: mark keywords, summary statements and important passages.</li>\n<li><strong>Look over the text quickly</strong> first.</li>\n<li><strong>Read the whole paragraph before you highlight</strong>: the <strong>summary statement</strong> (the sentence that gives the main point) is not always the first one.</li>\n<li>Notice <strong>summary words</strong>: <em>therefore, as a result, hence, in short, in conclusion</em>. The conclusion usually follows them.</li>\n<li>Circle <strong>keywords</strong> and difficult <strong>jargon</strong> (the special words of a subject), and <strong>infer</strong> (work out) a hard word’s meaning from its <strong>context</strong> (the words around it).</li>\n<li><strong>Paraphrase</strong>: say the idea in your own words.</li>\n<li><strong>Engage with the text</strong>: ask questions. Who is the main character? What issue does it address? What is the author telling you? Are the points compelling? Is the author biased?</li>\n</ol>\n<p>In a test: <strong>budget</strong> your time (less for short or easy passages, more for long or hard ones); <strong>skim</strong> (read the headings and the first lines); do the <strong>easiest</strong> exercises first, by finding the question’s <strong>keywords or their synonyms</strong> in the passage; read word by word only for the hard questions; guess hard words from context; never get stuck on one question. The two challenges are a weak vocabulary and too little practice. The cure: read more and widely, start with what you like, read something different every day, note down new words, and read a text more than once.</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Guessing <em>woolly</em> (the lecture’s sentence)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Many educationalists consider it a weak and <strong>woolly</strong> field, too far removed from the practical applications of the real world to be useful.”</p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>Woolly</em> is joined to <em>weak</em> by <em>and</em>, so it is probably close to <em>weak</em> in meaning.</li>\n<li>The rest of the sentence criticises the field: it is too far from the real world to be useful.</li>\n<li>So the word has a negative sense here.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> here <em>woolly</em> means vague, unclear.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w06/the-rules-for-comprehension\">the rules</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w06/comprehension-in-a-test\">comprehension in a test</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w06/challenges-and-how-to-improve\">challenges and how to improve</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"critical-reading-facts-propaganda-and-reasoning\">Critical reading: facts, propaganda and reasoning</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Fact and opinion</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>fact</strong> can be proved true with <strong>objective evidence</strong> (data, records, measurement): <em>China is the most populous country in the world</em> was the lecture’s fact, a statement data can check (by today’s data, India has passed China). An <strong>opinion</strong> is a belief, judgement or conclusion that cannot be objectively proved: <em>The best treatment for thyroid is homeopathy.</em> Much of what we read is a mixture of the two.</p>\n</div></section>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Propaganda</strong> is biased or misleading information used to promote a political cause or point of view. Its commonest technique is <strong>transfer</strong>: a product or a candidate links itself to something people love (the national flag, a famous film actor), so that the good feeling transfers to it.</li>\n<li>A <strong>fallacy</strong> is an error in reasoning. In <strong>circular reasoning</strong> the reason only repeats the conclusion: <em>Mahesh is a great swimmer, because he is so wonderful at swimming.</em> <strong>False cause</strong> assumes that because B came after A, A caused B: <em>I had a dosa instead of a paratha, and there was a major earthquake in my city.</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w06/critical-reading-facts-propaganda-and-reasoning\">facts, propaganda and reasoning</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"skimming-and-scanning\">Skimming and scanning</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Skimming</th>\n<th>Scanning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Purpose</td>\n<td>the overall picture, “an eagle’s eye view”</td>\n<td>one specific piece of information</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>You look at</td>\n<td>contents, index, headings, first and last lines, bold words, summaries</td>\n<td>one keyword at a time; you stop there and read around it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>At a fair of 100 stalls (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qby48_n3H_w&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>)</td>\n<td>you glance at all the stalls</td>\n<td>you look for one friend</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Use it</td>\n<td>to preview or to review a text</td>\n<td>to find a fact or an answer</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Skim first, then scan. Scanning needs focus, so choose a quiet place and a time when you are fresh.</p>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w06/skimming-and-scanning\">skimming and scanning</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sq3r-and-pqrst\">SQ3R and PQRST</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>SQ3R (Robinson, <em>Effective Study</em>, 1946)</th>\n<th>PQRST</th>\n<th>Done for</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Survey</td>\n<td>Preview</td>\n<td>the whole chapter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Question</td>\n<td>Question</td>\n<td>each section</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Read</td>\n<td>Read</td>\n<td>each section</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Recite</td>\n<td>State (self-recite)</td>\n<td>each section</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Review</td>\n<td>Test</td>\n<td>the whole chapter</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Survey</strong> or <strong>Preview</strong>: look at the title, the introduction, the headings, the illustrations and the footnotes: the map of the terrain before you walk in.</li>\n<li><strong>Question</strong>: turn every heading into a question. Water poured on a stone runs off, but a sponge soaks it up: a curious mind is like the sponge.</li>\n<li><strong>Read</strong> part by part (<strong>chunk</strong> the text into small sections), looking for the answers.</li>\n<li><strong>Recite</strong> or <strong>State</strong>: after each section, answer your questions from memory, aloud; check with the book; do not go on until you can.</li>\n<li><strong>Review</strong> or <strong>Test</strong>: at the end of the chapter, answer all the questions again and reread what you missed. “That which you measure grows.”</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w06/sq3r\">SQ3R</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w06/pqrst\">PQRST</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"short-texts-long-texts-and-the-critical-reader\">Short texts, long texts and the critical reader</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Short text</th>\n<th>Long text</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Content</td>\n<td>one key idea</td>\n<td>many chapters, many ideas</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The danger</td>\n<td>being laid back</td>\n<td>being overwhelmed and giving up</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>How to read</td>\n<td>full focus, like a child enjoying every bite of a chocolate</td>\n<td>“slice by slice”, as you would eat an elephant: chunk it, find your pace, then plan a schedule</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Notes</td>\n<td>the key idea; how it was introduced, supported and concluded</td>\n<td>after every chapter: keywords, terms, concepts, diagrams</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Feynman’s test: if you cannot explain a concept to a child in the fifth class without big words, perhaps you have not understood it.</p>\n<p><strong>The critical reader</strong> (6.5) puts in conscious, focused effort to understand a text in depth: prepares by reading about the author; keeps an <strong>open mind</strong> (“the parachute has a smooth landing only when it opens”); dwells on the <strong>title</strong>; looks up the <strong>vocabulary and terms</strong>; <strong>writes</strong> notes; makes <strong>connections</strong> with what they already know; and goes <strong>deep</strong>, beyond the eagle’s eye view.</p>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w06/shorter-and-longer-texts\">short and long texts</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w06/critical-reading-the-critical-reader-s-habits\">the critical reader’s habits</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"reading-tests\">Reading tests</h3>\n<p>IELTS reading has <strong>40 questions in 60 minutes</strong>; the Academic test has three long passages that get harder. Spend at most <strong>15, 20 and 25 minutes</strong> on sections 1, 2 and 3. Do not read the whole passage first: read one question, scan for its answer, then go to the next. In the lecture’s lists, <strong>ordered</strong> questions follow the order of the passage: missing words, True/False, and Yes/No/Not given (except in section 3). Multiple choice, matching paragraphs, sentence completion and every question of section 3 are not ordered; for <em>match the following</em>, the paragraph number is given. (The official IELTS descriptions put multiple choice, sentence completion and short-answer questions in text order. Know the lecture’s list for this course, and the official one for a real IELTS test.) Correct answers are <strong>paraphrased</strong>: an option that repeats the passage’s exact words is often a trap. The result is a <strong>band score</strong> of up to 9; for the same band, the General test needs more correct answers than the Academic test, because its questions are easier.</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Why one stuck question costs so much</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Time for each question: 60 ÷ 40 = 1.5 minutes.</li>\n<li>Six minutes on one question is the time of 6 ÷ 1.5 = 4 questions.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> you risk 4 marks to win 1. Mark the question, move on, and come back if time is left.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w06/preparing-for-a-reading-test\">preparing for a reading test</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w06/which-way-of-reading-when\">which way of reading, when</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Common mistakes in Week 6</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>Starting word by word: skim first, answer the easy questions, then read closely.</li>\n<li>Highlighting the first sentence before reading the whole paragraph.</li>\n<li>Jumping over unknown words: infer them from the context, or look them up.</li>\n<li>Mixing the two words: in rule 3 of Lecture 6.1, “scanning” means a quick look over; keep Lecture 6.2’s meanings: <strong>skim</strong> for the big picture, <strong>scan</strong> for one keyword.</li>\n<li>Taking an opinion (with words like <em>best</em>, <em>worst</em>, <em>should</em>) for a fact.</li>\n<li>Choosing the option that copies the passage’s words: look for the same <strong>meaning</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>Full revision:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w06\">Week 6</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"week-7-listening-active-and-passive-barriers-long-talks-and-\">Week 7: Listening: active and passive, barriers, long talks and tests</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Listening is half of communication: “We have been given one mouth and two ears.” This week compares two ways of listening, names the barriers to good listening and how to remove them, shows how to follow a long technical talk, practises <strong>predictive listening</strong> with a story, and gives tactics for listening tests.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"active-and-passive-listening\">Active and passive listening</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Passive and active listening</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Passive listening</strong> is close to just <strong>hearing</strong> (sound reaching your ears, with nothing done with it): no curiosity, no response, no <strong>feedback</strong> (the signals you send back to the speaker).</li>\n<li><strong>Active listening</strong> is “a very conscious attempt to understand whatever is being told …, to <strong>empathize</strong> with it, to learn what can be learned from it, to be curious enough to get involved and to give <strong>feedback</strong>” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KfE9W6SrP4w&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>). To empathise is to understand and share another person’s feelings.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Passive</th>\n<th>Active</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Mind</td>\n<td>disconnected, “just sitting there like a statue”</td>\n<td>“here and now”, curious</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Signals</td>\n<td>none</td>\n<td>eye contact, nods, <em>hmm, okay, really? and then?</em>, questions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Effort</td>\n<td>low</td>\n<td>“far more demanding”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Right for</td>\n<td>an announcement not meant for you, a formal speech, a flight safety talk</td>\n<td>a class, training, a sales talk, a leader with the team, a friend’s story</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Passive listening “certainly has its space”: at a formal function you listen without nodding and saying <em>oh, yes, I agree</em>. Questions give feedback <strong>both ways</strong>: the speaker learns that you are involved, and you learn whether you understood correctly. Active listening brings <strong>trust</strong>, helps resolve <strong>conflicts</strong>, prevents <strong>miscommunication</strong>, lets you <strong>foresee problems</strong>, grows your <strong>knowledge</strong>, and makes better <strong>leaders</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w07/active-and-passive-listening\">active and passive listening</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w07/six-benefits-of-active-listening\">six benefits</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"barriers-to-listening\">Barriers to listening</h3>\n<p>An old man tells his doctor that his wife is losing her hearing. At home he asks her the same question four times, coming closer each time; she turns round and says she has answered him three times. It was he who could not hear. “If not the problem, at least the solution lies within us.”</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Barrier</th>\n<th>How to remove it</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Physical distractions: traffic, noise, people coming and going</td>\n<td>choose your seat in advance; shut the window; ask people politely to move</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The speaker as a distraction: mannerisms, looks, style</td>\n<td>notice it, bring yourself back to the topic, take notes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Assumptions, like the man who hears the lion roar at the start of a film and says, “I have seen this movie”</td>\n<td>“I know about this topic; I am curious what this person brings to it”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Interruptions: by you, by others, by the speaker</td>\n<td>save your questions; politely ask for questions at the end</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Judging the speaker too soon</td>\n<td>park your judgement; stay a neutral listener till the end</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Defensive communication: feeling attacked, needing to prove you are right</td>\n<td>step back, notice it, and hear the speaker out</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The lecture does not say “never question or disagree”. The point is <strong>timing</strong>: “Can we give the benefit of doubt to the speaker until they are done?”</p>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w07/barriers-to-effective-listening\">barriers</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w07/outside-us-inside-us\">outside us, inside us</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"long-technical-talks\">Long technical talks</h3>\n<p>Listening is “<strong>hear and now</strong>”: be <strong>here</strong>, <strong>hear</strong>, and be <strong>now</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7NHotGqBQlM&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>).</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Before the talk</th>\n<th>During the talk</th>\n<th>After the talk</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>get alert: “whatever is your black coffee”</td>\n<td>note the key concepts, or every word (<strong>verbatim</strong>)</td>\n<td>summarise what you heard</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>read a little about the topic and the speaker; learn a few keywords</td>\n<td>note the keywords and your <strong>doubts</strong></td>\n<td>test yourself</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>brainstorm</strong> (jot down) what you already know</td>\n<td>a podcast can be paused; a lecturer cannot, so park your doubts for the end</td>\n<td>see the larger picture, and check your understanding</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w07/listening-to-longer-technical-talks\">longer technical talks</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"predictive-listening\">Predictive listening</h3>\n<p>Lecture 7.4 is a story, <em>Mohini and Ajeevika</em>. A 7-year-old girl whom nobody listens to defeats a monster: she gets him to copy her dance, and when he puts his own hands on his head, he laughs until he is exhausted; then she makes friends with him. Stories lower anxiety and stress. Good listening needs <strong>focus</strong>, <strong>no distractions</strong> and a <strong>relaxed</strong> mind.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Predictive listening</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>While you listen, your mind <strong>imagines</strong> the scene and <strong>predicts</strong> what may come next: “Is there going to be danger? Is there going to be success?” Each guess makes you curious to know which option is right, so you engage with what comes next and stay open to it.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w07/effective-listening-a-sample-practice\">the sample practice</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"listening-tests\">Listening tests</h3>\n<p>IELTS listening has <strong>40 questions in 4 sections</strong>, the same for the General and the Academic test; <strong>30 correct answers give band 7</strong>, and <strong>35 give band 8</strong>. The main difficulty is the lack of time.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Read all the questions of a section before the recording; use the gaps to read the next set.</li>\n<li>Watch <strong>two questions</strong> at a time: this one and the next.</li>\n<li>Mark the keywords in long options, and strike off options as soon as you know they are wrong.</li>\n<li><strong>Predict</strong> each blank: a noun, a verb or an adjective? A number, a street name or a colour?</li>\n<li>Write names, spellings and phone numbers <strong>at once</strong>. Speakers say zero as “o”: <em>double o</em> = 00, <em>triple o</em> = 000 (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=38w3ysMtrVw&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>).</li>\n<li>For a map, find the <strong>starting point</strong> before the recording starts.</li>\n<li>Use the last <strong>two minutes</strong> to check spelling and grammar.</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Write the number (my number)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>You hear: “eight one double o, six triple o, four two”.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>eight → 8; one → 1; double o → 00.</li>\n<li>six → 6; triple o → 000.</li>\n<li>four → 4; two → 2.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 8100600042 (10 digits). Count the zeros as you write them.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w07/how-to-ace-listening-tests\">how to ace listening tests</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Common mistakes in Week 7</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>Taking hearing for listening: listening means doing something with what you hear.</li>\n<li>Holding back questions out of shyness: questions give feedback both ways.</li>\n<li>Blaming only the room or the speaker: most barriers are inside us.</li>\n<li>Deciding from the title that you know it all (the assumption barrier).</li>\n<li>Interrupting too often in your eagerness to be an active listener.</li>\n<li>In a test: losing the next answer while you are stuck on one; writing “o” as the letter O.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>Full revision:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w07\">Week 7</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"week-8-syllables-word-stress-plurals-and-aspiration\">Week 8: Syllables, word stress, plurals and aspiration</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Week 8 returns to sounds, now inside words. Between a sound and a word sits the <strong>syllable</strong>. In a longer word, one syllable carries the <strong>stress</strong>. The <strong>plural</strong> ending changes its sound with the last sound of the word. And p, t and k get a puff of air in stressed syllables (<strong>aspiration</strong>, from Week 1). These patterns help you follow other speakers and choose how you speak.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"syllables\">Syllables</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Syllable</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A unit of a word, built around a <strong>nucleus</strong> (almost always a vowel <strong>sound</strong>), with optional <strong>margins</strong> (consonants) before and after it. Every syllable needs a vowel, so every word needs one. <em>Sun</em> is one syllable: the vowel is its nucleus, and s and n are its margins.</p>\n</div></section>\n<ul>\n<li>Count vowel <strong>sounds</strong>, not vowel letters: <em>boys</em> /bɔɪz/ is <strong>one</strong> syllable (one diphthong), and so is <em>know</em>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>jaw-drop test</strong>: put your hand under your jaw and say the word; count how often the jaw drops. <em>Be-gin</em>: 2. <em>In-for-ma-tion</em>: 4.</li>\n<li>A <strong>monosyllabic</strong> word has one syllable, a <strong>disyllabic</strong> word two, a <strong>polysyllabic</strong> word many. Words of five or more syllables are rare: <em>lex-i-co-gra-pher</em> has 5.</li>\n<li>Lecture 8.5 also practises the clusters of Week 1, by sound: two consonants at the start (<em>place, tray, dwell</em>) and at the end (<em>pump, help, adopt</em>); three at the start (<em>spring, straight, scream</em>) and at the end (<em>hands, solves, adopts</em>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w08/syllables\">syllables</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w08/counting-syllables\">counting syllables</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"word-stress\">Word stress</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Stress</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Prominence</strong>: one syllable is said with more force, a little <strong>louder</strong> and <strong>longer</strong> than the others: <em>WA-ter</em>. The three golden rules: a word has only <strong>one</strong> stress; only <strong>vowels</strong> are stressed; there are <strong>many exceptions</strong>. The patterns below hold for about 80 percent of the words in their class (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6SrSSSEHWxk&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>). A dictionary marks the stressed syllable with ˈ before it: <em>animation</em> /anɪˈmeɪʃ(ə)n/ is a-ni-<strong>MA</strong>-tion.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>A <strong>suffix</strong> is a part added at the end of a word (<em>-tion, -able</em>); a <strong>prefix</strong> is a part added at the start (<em>un-, pre-</em>).</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Pattern</th>\n<th>Stress on</th>\n<th>The lectures’ words</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>two-syllable nouns and adjectives</td>\n<td>the 1st syllable</td>\n<td>TI-ger, BREAK-fast, HAP-py</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>two-syllable verbs and prepositions</td>\n<td>the 2nd syllable</td>\n<td>be-GIN, re-LAX, a-MONG</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>one spelling, a noun and a verb</td>\n<td>noun 1st, verb 2nd</td>\n<td>an OB-ject, to ob-JECT; a DE-crease, to de-CREASE</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a word built from a one-syllable word</td>\n<td>the original word</td>\n<td>BREAK-able, FRIEND-ly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>compound nouns</td>\n<td>the 1st part</td>\n<td>BOOK-shop, TOOTH-paste, DIN-ing room</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>compound adjectives; phrasal verbs</td>\n<td>the 2nd word</td>\n<td>rock-SOLID; break DOWN, look OUT</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>reflexive pronouns</td>\n<td>the 2nd syllable</td>\n<td>my-SELF, them-SELVES</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>-tion, -sion, -cian, -ic</em></td>\n<td>the second-last syllable</td>\n<td>e-du-CA-tion, per-MIS-sion, rea-LIS-tic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>-able, -ary, -ible, -ical, -ics, -ion, -ial, -ity, -logy</em> and others</td>\n<td>the syllable just before the suffix</td>\n<td>un-der-STAND-a-ble, VIS-i-ble, me-MO-ri-al</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>-er, -or, -ly</em></td>\n<td>the 1st syllable</td>\n<td>MA-nag-er, OR-der-ly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>-ee, -oo, -ese, -ique, -ette</em></td>\n<td>the ending itself</td>\n<td>a-GREE, sham-POO, u-NIQUE</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>prefixes</td>\n<td>usually no stress (but <em>un, in, pre, ex, mis</em> take it)</td>\n<td>IN-efficient</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Exceptions are everywhere, even among the slides’ examples: dictionaries give di-REC-tor, con-TAIN-er and po-LICE, and they put the main stress of <em>inefficient</em> later (in-ef-FI-cient), with only a lighter stress on the prefix. The lecture’s advice: <strong>when in doubt, do not stress</strong>. No stress sounds better than a wrong stress.</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Where is the stress in <em>understandable</em>? (the lecture’s word)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Split it into syllables: un-der-stand-a-ble (5 syllables).</li>\n<li>Find the suffix: <em>-able</em>. It puts the stress on the syllable just before it.</li>\n<li>The syllable just before <em>-able</em> is <em>stand</em>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> un-der-STAND-a-ble.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>One spelling can serve as a noun and a verb because the stress tells them apart: the lecture calls this the <strong>principle of economy</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w08/word-stress\">word stress</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w08/two-syllable-words-nouns-and-adjectives-first-verbs-second\">two-syllable words</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w08/same-spelling-noun-or-verb\">noun or verb</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w08/endings-that-decide-the-stress\">endings that decide the stress</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w08/when-you-are-not-sure\">when you are not sure</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"plural-sounds\">Plural sounds</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The plural sound</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Listen to the <strong>last sound</strong> of the singular (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=42hufxqiOAk&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>after /p/, /t/ or /k/, the plural sounds <strong>/s/</strong>: <em>caps, cats, books, clocks</em>;</li>\n<li>everywhere else, it sounds <strong>/z/</strong>: <em>dogs, friends, babies, windows</em>;</li>\n<li>after the last sound of <em>judge</em>, <em>bench</em> or <em>rose</em>, a short vowel comes in to break up the consonants: <strong>/iz/</strong>: <em>judges, benches, roses</em>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>/s/ and /z/ differ only in <strong>voicing</strong> (/z/ buzzes). The spelling still writes <em>s</em>. <em>Teeth, oxen</em> and <em>children</em> are old plurals left over from Old English.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w08/plurals-s-z-and-iz\">plurals</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w08/the-rule\">the rule</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"aspiration\">Aspiration</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Rules for aspiration</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The <strong>voiceless stops</strong> /p/, /t/, /k/ (sounds made by blocking the air and letting it go, with no buzz) get a puff of air, written [pʰ], [tʰ], [kʰ] (8.5, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LdteKj-oE5I&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>at the start of a <strong>stressed syllable</strong>; in a one-syllable word, that is the start of the word: [tʰ]ake, po-[tʰ]A-to, de-[kʰ]REASE (the verb);</li>\n<li><strong>not</strong> at the start of an unstressed syllable (DE-crease, the noun), not at the end of a syllable (<em>park</em>, <em>deeper</em>), and not after /s/ (<em>stop, school</em>);</li>\n<li>the voiced stops b, d and g are <strong>never</strong> aspirated.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Lecture 8.5’s first rule also gives a puff at the start of any word; the clear puff comes at the start of a stressed syllable.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>Indian English</strong> usually does not aspirate, and that is a variety, not a mistake. Learn the pattern to understand other speakers, and copy it only once you understand it.</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Which words have a puff: <em>stop, pot, cot, oppose, tame</em>? (Lecture 8.5’s practice)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><em>pot, cot, tame</em>: p, k and t start the word and its only syllable → aspirated.</li>\n<li><em>oppose</em> is op-POSE: its p starts the stressed syllable → aspirated.</li>\n<li><em>stop</em>: the t comes after s → not aspirated.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> pot, cot, oppose and tame.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w08/aspiration\">aspiration</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w08/the-rules-to-remember\">the rules to remember</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w08/practice-from-lecture-8-5\">practice from Lecture 8.5</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Common mistakes in Week 8</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>Counting vowel letters: <em>boys</em> has one syllable; the silent e of <em>solves</em> is no vowel (the word ends in the sounds l, v, z).</li>\n<li>Stressing a syllable on purpose when you are not sure: stress none.</li>\n<li>Saying the noun and the verb alike: an OB-ject, to ob-JECT.</li>\n<li>A hissing /s/ at the end of <em>dogs</em>: the sound is /z/.</li>\n<li>A puff after s (<em>spy, stop</em>), or on b, d, g.</li>\n<li>Treating the stress patterns as laws: check new words in a dictionary.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>Full revision:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w08\">Week 8</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"week-9-presentations-group-discussions-storytelling-and-spea\">Week 9: Presentations, group discussions, storytelling and speaking tests</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Speaking for study and work. A good presentation has three elements: the <strong>message</strong>, the <strong>slides</strong> and the <strong>delivery</strong> (Lecture 9.1 means a typical 15-minute talk to a small, specialised audience, not public speaking). Then the <strong>group discussion</strong> used in placements, the power of <strong>storytelling</strong>, and how to do well in a <strong>speaking test</strong>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"scripting-a-presentation\">Scripting a presentation</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">From research to script</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Research</strong> (from reliable sources such as journals, newspapers and books) → <strong>brainstorm</strong> and draw a <strong>mind map</strong> (the topic in the middle, lines to the ideas around it) → find your <strong>core message</strong> (the one sentence the audience should take away) → <strong>outline</strong> (opening + body + conclusion) → write a rough <strong>draft</strong> → edit it with <strong>CED</strong>: keep what is <strong>C</strong>ritical and <strong>E</strong>ssential, and cut what is only <strong>D</strong>esirable, “regardless of how beautiful” it is. Keep it short: <strong>1,200 to 1,800 words for a 10- to 15-minute talk</strong>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Both ends of that range give the same speed: 1,200 ÷ 10 = 120 and 1,800 ÷ 15 = 120 words a minute.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Strategy and its order</th>\n<th>Best for</th>\n<th>The lecture’s example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>PREP</strong>: Position, Reason, Example, Position again</td>\n<td>a debatable point</td>\n<td>germline gene editing should wait</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Past, present, future</strong>: in time order</td>\n<td>informing and predicting</td>\n<td>the Fast-Track Plan</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Problem, cause, solution</strong>: a solution for each cause</td>\n<td>business and policy</td>\n<td>FletNix in India</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>AIDA</strong>: Attention, Interest, Desire, Action</td>\n<td>persuading: adverts, sales pitches</td>\n<td>the Grammarly advert</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>PREP’s openers: <em>I think …</em> (position), <em>Firstly …</em> (reason), <em>For instance …</em> (example), <em>Clearly …</em> (position again).</p>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w09/scripting-a-presentation\">scripting a presentation</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w09/four-scripting-strategies\">four strategies</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w09/which-strategy-when\">which strategy when</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"slides-and-delivery\">Slides and delivery</h3>\n<p>Slides must be <strong>functional, uncluttered and effective</strong>; you do not have to be a great designer.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>slide deck</strong> (the set of slides) needs a cover, an <strong>overview</strong> and a <strong>summary</strong> (the key message and a <strong>call for action</strong>: what you want the audience to do); a thank-you slide is preferable; textual, visual, chart and section-header slides as needed (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=225s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:45</a>). At most <strong>six bullets</strong> a slide.</li>\n<li>Images: only from <strong>Creative Commons</strong> (free to use), otherwise <strong>cite</strong> the owner.</li>\n<li><strong>Two fonts</strong>: one <strong>serif</strong> (letters with small “feet”; for long text, like Times New Roman) and one <strong>sans serif</strong> (no feet; for headlines, like Arial). No informal fonts such as Comic Sans. About <strong>three colours</strong>. Tidy tables: round the values, number the rows, highlight the total.</li>\n<li>Don’t rely on your slides, don’t dump text on them, and adapt them to your audience.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Delivery:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Before</strong>: rehearse (with a friend, a mirror, “or even your pet”); check that slides and script are in sync; keep your outline with you; go early; know where the camera or audience is; dress well; breathe; <strong>speak slowly and clearly</strong>; <strong>finish on time</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Open</strong> with a <strong>rhetorical question</strong> (asked for effect, not for an answer), an attention-grabbing fact, a short story, or the purpose and outline. <strong>Close</strong> with a summary and a call for action.</li>\n<li><strong>After</strong>: invite questions; break a long question into parts; <strong>respond, don’t react</strong> (think, then answer calmly); if you do not know the answer, say so.</li>\n<li>The takeaways: preparation is key; you only need to know where to look; <strong>minimalism wins</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w09/designing-the-slides\">designing the slides</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w09/serif-and-sans-serif-fonts\">serif and sans serif fonts</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w09/delivering-the-presentation\">delivering the presentation</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"group-discussions\">Group discussions</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Group discussion (GD)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Usually <strong>8 to 9</strong> similarly qualified people discuss a topic, a problem or a case, and try to reach a <strong>solution</strong> or a <strong>consensus</strong> (an agreement of the whole group). In placements it comes between the <strong>résumé shortlist</strong> and the <strong>interview</strong>. It shows interpersonal skills, listening, a crisp core message, knowledge, creativity and spontaneity, and the candidate’s <strong>value-fit</strong> with the company. A <strong>focus group discussion</strong> (FGD) is used in market research and social research.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The four types of topic: <strong>general</strong> (no two sides: <em>Small businesses are an important lifeline of India’s cities</em>); <strong>argumentative</strong> (two sides, and the commonest: <em>A university education is not worth pursuing in India today</em>); <strong>abstract</strong> (philosophical, open to any reading: <em>Change is the only constant in existence</em>); <strong>caselets</strong> (a short real case to solve: a streaming platform that is too costly and not local enough for India).</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">The ten minutes (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p4iFKPK1e14&amp;t=130s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:10</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>The panel of 3 judges reads out the topic: 1 minute. The leader sets the direction: 1 minute. The members take it forward: 7 minutes. The leader sums up: 1 minute.</li>\n<li>Total: 1 + 1 + 7 + 1 = 10 minutes.</li>\n<li>With 9 members, 8 share the 7 minutes: 7 × 60 = 420 seconds, and 420 ÷ 8 = 52.5 seconds each.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> about one minute each, or a little less, so make one clear point.</p>\n</div></section>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Leading</strong>: volunteer only if you have a meaningful point: <em>May I start with the permission of the chair and the group?</em> Give a <strong>topic statement</strong> (a one-sentence summary of the topic), the general opinion, your own view and a <strong>direction</strong>; take notes throughout; add <strong>no new points</strong> in the summary.</li>\n<li><strong>Taking part</strong>: grasp the central point and stick to it; jot down 2 or 3 points; in about a minute give a clear topic statement, one or two points, your stance, and perhaps a question for the group (PREP-style).</li>\n<li><strong>Phrases</strong>: making a point, <em>One point that I want to make is that …</em>; disagreeing, <em>I beg to differ, but …</em>; agreeing, <em>I second your opinion, and would also like to add that …</em>; agreeing with a doubt, <em>I see where you’re coming from and I agree, but …</em>; interrupting, <em>Can I add something here?</em></li>\n<li><strong>Don’ts</strong>: do not repeat others, look bored, merely agree, dominate (“this is a discussion, not a debate”), or make personal, sexist or casteist remarks.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w09/group-discussions\">group discussions</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w09/rules-in-a-gd-leading-and-taking-part\">rules, leading and taking part</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w09/useful-phrases\">useful phrases</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"storytelling\">Storytelling</h3>\n<p>Oral storytelling is “as old as speech itself”. Its power lies in the <strong>content</strong> (a story hooks our attention and stays in memory) and in the <strong>presentation</strong> (tone, face, gestures: “90 percent of our communication is not what we say, it is how we say it”). The lecture’s story: a boy goes alone to buy a Pongal shirt with Amma’s rule, “quote half the price”: 100 → 50, 80 → 40, 50 → 25, 30 → 15; when the shopkeeper says “Just take it for free!”, the boy asks for two shirts.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Licensed and channelized daydreaming</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Storytelling is the lecturer’s “licensed and channelized daydreaming”. <strong>Licensed</strong>: when someone tells you a story, you are allowed to daydream. <strong>Channelized</strong>: the storyteller guides the daydream along one path. “The shortest distance between two humans is a story”, and “a friend is one whose story you know”.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w09/the-art-of-storytelling\">the art of storytelling</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"speaking-tests\">Speaking tests</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Part</th>\n<th>What happens</th>\n<th>Time</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1. Introduction</td>\n<td>you, your family, work, interests, hobbies</td>\n<td>4 to 5 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2. General topic</td>\n<td>a topic card, <strong>1 minute</strong> to prepare, then you speak</td>\n<td>3 to 4 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3. Conversation</td>\n<td>the examiner’s questions on the topic</td>\n<td>4 to 5 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>You are scored on <strong>fluency, vocabulary, grammar and pronunciation</strong>; an Indian accent is no problem if you are fluent. Avoid repetition, <strong>clichés</strong> (overused words: <em>very, awesome</em>), <strong>slang</strong> (very informal words: <em>chill, dude</em>), swear words, memorised answers, and local words the examiner may not know (say <em>wheat bread</em>, not <em>roti</em>). In Part 2, make bullet notes in the minute; keep a steady pace; never give one-word answers; and relax, as if you were talking to a fellow passenger on a train.</p>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w09/how-to-ace-speaking-tests\">how to ace speaking tests</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Common mistakes in Week 9</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>Putting everything you know into the script: cut what is only desirable.</li>\n<li>Using AIDA for an informative talk: it is a method of persuasion.</li>\n<li>Crowded slides, many fonts and colours, images without credit.</li>\n<li>Reacting angrily to a hard question, or bluffing an answer.</li>\n<li>In a GD: volunteering to lead with no point to make; adding new points in the summary; repeating others; dominating.</li>\n<li>In a speaking test: “Biryani. Awesome.” is a one-word answer with a cliché and an unexplained local word.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>Full revision:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w09\">Week 9</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"week-10-common-errors-and-writing-basics\">Week 10: Common errors and writing basics</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Two lectures on errors that even educated users make, then four on writing, all by Prof. Shreesh Chaudhary. Most errors come from <strong>over-generalisation</strong>: using a pattern in places where English does not use it (“we say <em>eating</em>, so why can’t we say <em>seeming</em>?”). Most weak writing comes from writing <strong>more than necessary</strong>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"nouns-and-verbs-that-break-the-pattern\">Nouns and verbs that break the pattern</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Uncountable nouns</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>An <strong>uncountable</strong> (mass) noun takes <strong>no a/an</strong> and <strong>no -s or -es</strong>: <em>information, equipment, furniture, meat, oil, rice, wheat, milk, bread, petrol, sugar, tea, water, news</em>, and <em>fish</em> as food (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=545s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:05</a>). A dictionary marks it <strong>[U]</strong>; a countable noun is <strong>[C]</strong>. (A few of these also have a countable use for a cup, a spoonful or a kind: <em>Two teas, please</em>; <em>How many sugars?</em>) ✗ <em>We got some informations</em> → <em>We got some information.</em></p>\n</div></section>\n<p>To give an amount, use a <strong>quantifier</strong> (a word of quantity: <em>some, a little</em>) or a <strong>partitive</strong> (a phrase that names a part or a unit): <em>some information, a little knowledge, a news item, a loaf of bread, a litre of water, a grain of rice, a piece of furniture</em>. Why not <em>breads</em>, when Hindi counts <em>roti</em>? Languages are <strong>arbitrary</strong> (fixed by custom, not by logic), just as grammatical gender is.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Verbs that break the pattern</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>In the lecture’s rule, <em>appear, feel, have, look, seem, stand</em>, when they describe a <strong>state</strong>, take no <em>-ing</em>: ✗ <em>It is seeming correct</em> → <em>It seems correct</em>; ✗ <em>You are appearing to be tired</em> → <em>You appear to be tired</em>; ✗ <em>I am having a car</em> → <em>I have a car</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=1415s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:35</a>). In other meanings they can: <em>I am having breakfast</em> (eat), <em>Gopal is standing for election</em> (contest), <em>I am feeling the patient’s pulse</em> (touch). Standard English is less strict with <em>feel</em>, <em>look</em> and <em>stand</em>: <em>I’m feeling fine</em>, <em>You’re looking tired</em> and <em>A policeman is standing at the post</em> are correct.</li>\n<li><em>cast, cost, cut, put</em> have <strong>one form</strong> in all tenses: ✗ <em>Votes have been casted</em> → <em>Votes have been cast.</em> (They still take <em>-s</em> in the present: <em>This book costs 500 rupees.</em>)</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w10/common-errors-1-nouns-and-verbs-that-break-the-pattern\">common errors 1</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w10/talking-about-amounts\">talking about amounts</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w10/verbs-that-do-not-take-ing\">verbs that do not take -ing</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w10/verbs-with-one-form\">verbs with one form</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"agreement-with-the-head\">Agreement with the head</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The head of a noun phrase</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>noun phrase</strong> is a group of words built around one noun, its <strong>head</strong>; the other words in it are <strong>modifiers</strong> (they add information about the head). “Regardless of how many modifiers there are, it is always the head to which the verb agrees in number” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LNczGDRKX6Y&amp;t=717s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:57</a>). The usual error is to agree with the noun <strong>nearest</strong> the verb.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In noun + noun, the <strong>second</strong> noun is the head: <em>history <strong>teacher</strong></em>, <em>Home <strong>Minister</strong></em>.</li>\n<li>In noun + preposition + noun, the noun <strong>before</strong> the preposition is the head: <em><strong>Inspector</strong> of Police</em>; <em>The <strong>sons</strong> of Pandu <strong>were</strong> exiled.</em></li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Find the head (the lecture’s sentences)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><em>Some boys of Rajendra Hostel ___ making all the noise.</em> The head is <em>boys</em> (plural) → <strong>are</strong>.</li>\n<li><em>Only one of the six scheduled trains ___ running late this morning.</em> The head is <em>one</em> → <strong>is</strong>.</li>\n<li><em>The entire team with all its star players ___ been found guilty of doping.</em> The head is <em>team</em> → <strong>has</strong>.</li>\n<li><em>Puurna’s collection of old folk songs ___ likely to be published soon.</em> The head is <em>collection</em> → <strong>is</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> are, is, has, is. Cover the modifiers and read what is left.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w10/common-errors-2-subject-verb-agreement-and-the-head\">common errors 2</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w10/how-to-find-the-head\">how to find the head</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-makes-writing-good\">What makes writing good</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Writing lasts long and goes far</strong>: a written text is easy to read, re-read, revise and re-write, so business, science and literature live in writing (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EJN3-yzAhqU&amp;t=387s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:27</a>). Its basic technology never changes: a surface, symbols, tools to put the symbols on the surface, and the rules for their order and meaning.</li>\n<li><strong>Content comes first</strong>: “Do you have something to say?” Kabir Das is said to have been illiterate, and Shakespeare, the slide says, had hardly been to a primary school, yet both are read today. Gather your information before you write.</li>\n<li><strong>Small words and simple sentences</strong>: <em>Can you see the outlines of this house?</em>, not <em>Can you visualise the contours of the design of this project?</em> The King James Bible’s <em>And God said, let there be light: and there was light</em> is easier than an office memo about action “on the illumination front”.</li>\n<li><strong>Writing is a skill</strong>, like cycling: practise every day; “only you can teach yourself how to write”.</li>\n<li><strong>A good script comes after several drafts</strong> (a <strong>draft</strong> is one version before the final one): Hemingway and R. K. Narayan made up to a dozen. Poems and stories are free style; letters, reports, essays, mails and memos have fixed formats.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w10/what-makes-writing-good\">what makes writing good</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w10/the-basics-of-good-writing\">the basics of good writing</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"bio-notes-photographs-and-public-writing\">Bio-notes, photographs and public writing</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Bio-note</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A short note (often 50 to 200 words) that tells “what sort of a person the writer is”, without dates or places; those belong in the <strong>CV</strong> (the detailed record of your work and studies). Say what makes you <strong>different</strong>, failures or successes, written <strong>objectively</strong> (as facts, without praising yourself). A mission or vision statement is optional; if you state an aim, make it concrete. Not <em>I want a great career in management</em>, a <strong>cliché</strong> (a phrase repeated so often that it has lost its meaning), but <em>In the next five years I want to become a middle-level manager.</em></p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The enemies of good writing (10.4): <strong>redundancy</strong> (words that repeat what is already said), <strong>padding</strong> (words that only fill space) and <strong>verbosity</strong> (using far more words than needed). Use the <strong>accurate</strong> word: “only a particular button will fit a particular hole”.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Describing a photograph</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“In no more than 150 words, describe all that you see”: about 145 to 155 words. Look at everything first; describe only what is <strong>in</strong> the picture; give <strong>facts, not attitudes</strong>. Avoid <strong>subjective</strong> words (<em>very beautiful</em>: they depend on the writer’s feelings), clichés (<em>packed like fish in cans</em>), vague words (<em>lots of</em>: “your inability to count”) and jokes about the people in it.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Writing for public purposes</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A public text has no fixed reader, so it must be <strong>formal, factual, precise and brief</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=229s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:49</a>). A <strong>civic problem</strong> is a <strong>man-made</strong> problem: crime, corruption, chaos, pollution, poverty, a waterlogged road. Gather facts (numbers, local observations, quantities), describe the harm (to traffic, pedestrians, health, the look of the place), and <strong>suggest remedies</strong>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>The lecture’s sample</th>\n<th>First draft</th>\n<th>Final draft</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Baagmati’s bio-note</td>\n<td>278 words, 25 sentences</td>\n<td>155 words, 13 sentences</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The ladies’ compartment photograph</td>\n<td>192 words, 13 sentences</td>\n<td>146 words, 14 sentences</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The letter on waterlogging</td>\n<td>291 words, little information</td>\n<td>148 words, a lot of information</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">The shop notice (the lecture’s photograph)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>THIS STOR IS NOT OPEN TODAY BECOS IT IS CLOSED.. BY: MENEJA</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Spelling: <em>store, because, manager</em>.</li>\n<li>Conventions: capital letters all through, and two full stops.</li>\n<li>Redundancy: <em>not open today</em> and <em>closed</em> say the same thing.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>This store is closed today by order of the manager.</em> “Redundancy is a great enemy of all writing.”</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w10/bio-notes\">bio-notes</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w10/describing-a-photograph\">describing a photograph</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w10/what-the-final-draft-removed\">what the final draft removed</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w10/writing-for-public-purposes\">writing for public purposes</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Common mistakes in Week 10</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>✗ <em>informations, furnitures, breads, a news</em>: uncountable nouns take no <em>-s</em> and no <em>a</em>.</li>\n<li>✗ <em>It is seeming correct</em>, ✗ <em>I am having a car</em>: state verbs take no <em>-ing</em>.</li>\n<li>✗ <em>casted, costed, putted</em>.</li>\n<li>Agreeing with the nearest noun: ✗ <em>Some boys of Rajendra Hostel is making all the noise.</em></li>\n<li>Big words and long sentences to sound clever.</li>\n<li>The meaning of your name in a bio-note, <em>very beautiful</em> saris in a description, <em>traffic hazard</em> with no facts in a public letter.</li>\n<li>Going over the word limit by more than about 5 words.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>Full revision:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w10\">Week 10</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"week-11-notes-business-letters-instructions-data-reports-and\">Week 11: Notes, business letters, instructions, data reports and essays</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Eight lectures on practical writing, each taking a weak first draft to a better final draft. The method is always the same: <strong>plan your points first</strong>, keep “<strong>all and only</strong>” the facts the reader needs, put them in a fixed order with the <strong>most important first</strong>, and write short, simple sentences.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"notes-and-business-letters\">Notes and business letters</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Note</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A short, <strong>factual</strong> description, here about 150 words, of something you own. Plan a <strong>synopsis</strong> (the list of points) first: when you got it; what it looks like (colour, shape, size, weight); its special features; what you like best; any event linked to it. The lecture’s phone note went from <strong>385 words in 21 sentences</strong> to <strong>150 words in 12</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dObw3Ikv1FE&amp;t=896s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:56</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Accuracy means the one right word: <em>It can expand to 256GB</em> (not <em>can be expanded up to</em>); <em>a smaller power back up</em> (the comparative, because two phones are compared); <em>I do not like phones that need frequent charging</em> (<em>prefer</em> needs two things); a screen is a <strong>part</strong> of a phone, not an <em>extra</em>; <em>in a pant pocket</em> (one phone, one pocket).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Business writing</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>It makes three demands (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tm2Ou96Kajs&amp;t=578s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:38</a>). It is full of <strong>facts</strong> (what, when, where, how much, how many). It keeps a <strong>fixed form</strong>: “there is a place for everything and everything has a place”. And it follows a <strong>rank order</strong>: the most important thing first, the least important last.</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Part of a business letter</th>\n<th>The lost-bag letter</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>From</td>\n<td>Mira Lobo Prabhu, No. 31, Coondapur Road, Mangalore</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>To</td>\n<td>The Manager, Mangalagiri Transport Company, Railway Station Road, Mangalore</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Date</td>\n<td>20 Jan, 2021</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Salutation (greeting)</td>\n<td>Sir,</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Subject line</td>\n<td>Sub: Left Luggage - Reg. (<em>Sub</em> = subject; <em>Reg.</em> = regarding)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Body</td>\n<td>the bus, the time and the stops; the request; the bag; what is inside it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Closing and name</td>\n<td>With best regards, M L Prabhu</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The first draft (278 words) was correct English, full of feelings (“very dear to my heart”, “a sleepless night”) and almost empty of facts. The final draft (154 words) names the bus (<em>7 T</em>), the time (<em>about 05:35 pm</em>), the stops, <em>a blue foam leather lady’s bag</em> with <em>two brass clips</em>, and every item inside, so anyone can find it. Correct is not the same as effective.</p>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w11/note-writing\">note-writing</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w11/accuracy-the-one-right-word\">accuracy</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w11/business-writing-facts-form-and-order\">business writing</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w11/the-final-draft-154-words-all-facts\">the final lost-bag letter</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"instructions-and-personal-details\">Instructions and personal details</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Writing instructions</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Maps and plans need language for <strong>sequence</strong> (what comes first), <strong>directions</strong> (which way), <strong>numbers</strong> and <strong>options</strong> (the choices on the way) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eZYAswxzk7w&amp;t=178s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:58</a>). A mistake here is not forgiven like a slip of grammar: is it 13 or 30? An instruction must be clear, <strong>unambiguous</strong> (with only one possible meaning), precise and brief.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The metro letter, from the airport to Noida City Centre, went from 274 words to 153. The final draft cuts the chat about dinner, gives the total time first, and numbers the steps <strong>in the order they happen</strong>: buy a one-way ticket; <strong>keep the ticket and the token</strong> to the end; take the Orange Line to NDLS, change to the Yellow Line for one stop to Rajiv Chowk, then the Blue Line to Noida City Centre; “Take the Exit 1. I will be there.” A numbered exit is clearer than “turn left”: “who’s left, who’s right?” Addresses are not counted in the word limit.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Personal details in a business letter</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Ask your host <strong>in advance</strong>, in a business-like, formal and polite way; formal and polite do not conflict. The Blackpool letter’s first sentence does the most work (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IDpOwgM3aZo&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>): <em>I will reach Blackpool by the 4:30 train from London on 17 March.</em> It says where, how, by which train and on which day. Then come the room (<em>a room with a window</em>), the food (<em>vegetarian</em>), the sightseeing and the shopping, in order of importance. Cut boasting (<em>business class</em>), the obvious, and vague words (<em>good discounts</em>). The letter went from 243 words to 152.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w11/writing-instructions-maps-and-plans\">writing instructions</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w11/the-final-draft-numbered-steps\">the numbered steps</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w11/personal-details-in-a-business-letter\">personal details</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"data-reports\">Data reports</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">What a data report says</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Numbers make writing <strong>objective</strong> (based on facts anyone can check). A report on a table or a chart says (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kiv6xUsQsQ&amp;t=334s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:34</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>what the data are about, and for which period;</li>\n<li>how they are presented: a table, a bar chart, a histogram, a graph, a pie chart;</li>\n<li>the <strong>main trends</strong> (the general direction of the numbers);</li>\n<li>the <strong>significant details</strong>;</li>\n<li>the <strong>differences between nodes</strong> (points or groups in the data);</li>\n<li>a <strong>concluding remark</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Write about 150 words (5 more or less is fine). Do not copy every number (<strong>mechanical reproduction</strong>), and do not go off on a <strong>tangent</strong> (a sudden move to another subject).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Chunks for the household survey (the lecture’s pie chart)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The chart: housewife/unemployed 56%, domestic help 28%, tradesperson 7%, other service job 5%, self-employed 2%, labourer 2%.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Check the total: 56 + 28 + 7 + 5 + 2 + 2 = 100.</li>\n<li>56% is more than 50% → “<strong>over half</strong> the women”.</li>\n<li>28% is a little more than 25% → “<strong>a little more than one fourth</strong>”.</li>\n<li>Say what the chart does not tell: the number of respondents and the time of the survey.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> “Over half the women are housewives or unemployed; a little more than one fourth work as domestic help.” Write <em>over</em> for <em>more than</em>, <em>under</em> for <em>less than</em>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w11/data-interpretation-from-visual-to-verbal\">visual to verbal</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w11/what-a-data-report-must-say\">what a data report must say</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w11/a-data-report-the-household-survey\">the household survey</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"essays-and-simplicity\">Essays and simplicity</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Essay</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>An essay tests your language, your knowledge of the world, and whether you can order ideas from important to unimportant. It needs <strong>content</strong> (arguments, examples, data and evidence), <strong>planning</strong> (a synopsis and a chain of points) and <strong>examples</strong>. The lecture’s essay on agriculture went from 594 words to 252 by cutting irrelevant ideas, clichés (<em>the pathetic state of agriculture</em>), vague words (<em>a tremendous body of knowledge</em>), doubles (<em>roles and responsibilities</em>) and piles of pronouns (repeat the noun: <em>might bring <strong>farmers</strong> better prices</em>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The five rules for simple sentences</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>No sentence of <strong>more than 10 words</strong>.</li>\n<li>Only <strong>one verb</strong> in a sentence.</li>\n<li>Words of <strong>one or two syllables</strong>, unless unavoidable.</li>\n<li><strong>More than one draft</strong>.</li>\n<li>A <strong>dictionary and a thesaurus</strong> when in doubt.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>(<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nsKHDgllW0&amp;t=1558s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:58</a>)</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Great writers wrote simply: the Buddha, Shakespeare (<em>When shall we three meet again / In thunder, lightning, or in rain?</em>), Wordsworth, Dickens and Hawking. A <strong>simple</strong> sentence has one subject and one verb (<em>It saves time</em>); a <strong>compound</strong> sentence joins two simple ones with <em>and, but</em> or <em>or</em>; a <strong>complex</strong> sentence adds a part that depends on the main one (<em>I cook at home because it saves money</em>). Chat words (<em>LOL, chill bro</em>) are fine among friends; for the public, who also judge your applications, write formal, simple English.</p>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w11/writing-an-essay\">writing an essay</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w11/vague-words-cliche-s-and-jargon\">vague words, clichés and jargon</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w11/the-power-of-simplicity\">the power of simplicity</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w11/how-to-write-simple-sentences\">the five rules</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Common mistakes in Week 11</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>Feelings in place of facts, like the lost bag that was “very dear to my heart”.</li>\n<li>Steps out of order; “turn left”; a number that could be 13 or 30.</li>\n<li>A surprise request at the meeting, instead of in advance.</li>\n<li>Copying a chart’s numbers one by one, or making a speech about something else.</li>\n<li>✗ <em>Looking forward to see you</em>: after <em>look forward to</em>, use <em>-ing</em>: <em>Looking forward to seeing you</em>.</li>\n<li>Long sentences with many verbs: break them up.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>Full revision:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w11\">Week 11</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"week-12-professional-writing-paragraphs-formal-style-editing\">Week 12: Professional writing: paragraphs, formal style, editing and proofreading</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The last week asks what makes writing <strong>professional</strong> and how to produce it. Lecture 12.1 defines professional writing and names its four elements; Lectures 12.2 to 12.4 take the elements one by one; Lecture 12.5 gives tips for writing exams.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"what-professional-writing-is\">What professional writing is</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Professional writing</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“A style of writing used in delivering <strong>authentic</strong> information on a subject by following <strong>systematic rules</strong>” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mzIYctZ7HGg&amp;t=40s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:40</a>), for a <strong>specific audience</strong>, with a <strong>purpose</strong>: product catalogues, brochures, the description on a tablet strip, user manuals, workplace reports and emails. It is written so that readers can <strong>act</strong>. (<em>Authentic</em> = true and reliable.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Professional writing</th>\n<th>Literary writing</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Written to</td>\n<td>inform and instruct</td>\n<td>entertain and amuse</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Language</td>\n<td>direct, factual, specific; technical words; simple sentences</td>\n<td>figures of speech (hyperbole, metaphor), complex sentences, dialects</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tone</td>\n<td>impersonal and objective</td>\n<td>emotional</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Readers</td>\n<td>a specific audience</td>\n<td>general readers</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Its four elements: <strong>developed and organised paragraphs</strong>; an <strong>appropriate (formal) style</strong>; <strong>clarity of ideas</strong>; <strong>spelling and grammar</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w12/what-professional-writing-is\">what professional writing is</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w12/the-four-elements-of-professional-writing\">the four elements</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"building-a-paragraph\">Building a paragraph</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The three parts of a paragraph</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>The <strong>topic sentence</strong> makes an <strong>arguable claim</strong> (not a plain fact) and shows the paragraph’s purpose. It can be a complex sentence, a question, or a <strong>pivot</strong> that turns the direction (<em>However, …</em>). In professional writing it comes <strong>first</strong> (or second).</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting sentences</strong> give reasons; facts, data and statistics; quotations; examples.</li>\n<li>The <strong>concluding sentence</strong> restates or reinforces the point with a prediction, a suggestion, a recommendation, a summary or a final observation. It is <strong>required</strong> in a stand-alone paragraph, but not in every body paragraph of a longer text.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>(<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Unity and coherence</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Unity</strong> means one idea per paragraph. <strong>Coherence</strong> means each sentence leads logically to the next: the sentences are “tied together”. To unify a paragraph: keep the logic continuous, <strong>repeat the key words</strong>, and use <strong>transitional devices</strong> (linking words) to add (<em>furthermore, moreover</em>), to contrast (<em>however, nevertheless, although</em>) or to conclude (<em>to sum up, in conclusion</em>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Six ways to build an argument: an example; a story or anecdote (in personal writing); <strong>compare and contrast</strong> (for subtle differences only: two kinds of chairs, not a table and a chair); cause and effect; data and statistics; a definition. Real paragraphs <strong>embed</strong> several of them. In the lecture’s paragraph on Flemish comics, sentence 1 is the topic sentence, sentences 2 and 3 support it, sentence 4 adds a final observation; the key word <em>comic</em> keeps returning, and <em>Traditionally</em>, <em>For more than half a century</em> and <em>Today</em> link the sentences.</p>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w12/building-a-paragraph\">building a paragraph</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w12/unity-and-coherence\">unity and coherence</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w12/strategies-for-making-an-argument\">ways to make an argument</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"formal-style-and-objective-tone\">Formal style and objective tone</h3>\n<p>Formal style is <strong>less personal</strong> (not impersonal: an email keeps a little personal touch), it is used for professional or academic purposes, and its tone is <strong>objective</strong> (based on facts, not feelings). Formality is a <strong>range</strong>, set by the situation (the medium and the domain), the audience’s expectations and, above all, the <strong>purpose</strong>. What makes language <strong>informal</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=460s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:40</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Avoid</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>group jargon (words only one group uses)</td>\n<td><em>coords</em>, <em>the FR team</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>slang, idioms, colloquialisms, local words</td>\n<td><em>Y’all, ASAP, cool, yo</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>stereotypical and biased language</td>\n<td><em>chairman, mankind</em> → <em>chairperson, human beings</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>first- and second-person pronouns</td>\n<td><em>I, we, you</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>contractions</td>\n<td><em>I’ve, you’re</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>For clarity, also cut <strong>wordy phrases</strong> (<em>In my personal opinion</em> → <em>In my opinion</em>; <em>came to the realisation that</em> → <em>realised that</em>), <strong>clichés</strong> (<em>leave no stone unturned, last but not least</em>) and <strong>jargon</strong> (<em>low-hanging fruit, the bottom line</em>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Objective tone: five rules</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Put the emphasis on ideas, not people: <em>These findings indicate that the arguments are valid</em>, not <em>I think that the argument is valid</em>.</li>\n<li>Use no evaluative (judging) words without evidence.</li>\n<li>Avoid intense, emotional words: <em>The writing needs improvement</em>, not <em>terrible</em>; <em>well argued</em>, not <em>wonderful</em>.</li>\n<li>Use <strong>modality</strong> to leave room for disagreement: <em>There is a possibility that the policy works well for everyone.</em></li>\n<li>Cite <strong>authentic sources</strong> for credibility.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>(<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=1530s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:30</a>)</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w12/formal-style-and-clarity\">formal style and clarity</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w12/what-makes-language-informal\">what makes language informal</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w12/objective-tone\">objective tone</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"editing-and-proofreading\">Editing and proofreading</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Editing</th>\n<th>Proofreading</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Looks at</td>\n<td>the organisation (“logistic” errors)</td>\n<td>the surface</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Checks</td>\n<td><strong>content</strong> (is the question answered?), <strong>overall structure</strong> (an introduction and a conclusion, a logical order, visible transitions), <strong>clarity</strong> (defined terms; no vague, wordy, clichéd or ornate words), <strong>style</strong> (an objective tone; no <em>due to the fact that</em>)</td>\n<td>spelling, grammar, punctuation, format (citations, page numbers, fonts, spacing)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Proofread by reading <strong>aloud</strong>, checking <strong>your own</strong> list of errors first, looking for <strong>one error at a time</strong>, and checking the format. The <strong>14 common surface errors</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1030s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:10</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>No.</th>\n<th>Error</th>\n<th>Right</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>no comma after an introductory phrase</td>\n<td><em>According to the secretary, the files should be submitted in a sealed envelope.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>a vague pronoun</td>\n<td><em>The bus is blue in colour</em> (not <em>It is</em>, after a bus and a bike)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>no comma in a compound sentence</td>\n<td><em>I will write the email, and I will send the report to the manager.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>the wrong word</td>\n<td><em>take the advice</em> (the noun; <em>advise</em> is the verb)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>a wrong or missing verb ending</td>\n<td><em>The new staff member always leaves her desk unattended.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>a comma splice (two sentences joined by a comma)</td>\n<td><em>I am skilled at typing; I can type at a high speed.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>a missing or misplaced apostrophe</td>\n<td><em>Asha’s office desk</em>; <em>Officers should report …</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>a needless shift in tense</td>\n<td><em>The reports are presented to the clients; they are verified.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>a wrong shift in pronoun</td>\n<td><em>He was on his way back from work.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>a sentence fragment</td>\n<td><em>I want to attend the meeting.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>subject–verb agreement</td>\n<td><em>I like the ambience of my office.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12</td>\n<td>no commas in a series</td>\n<td><em>The reports of the previous meeting, files, and writing pads …</em> (the comma before <em>and</em> is the Oxford comma: the lecture asks for it, but it is a choice of style, and many British and Indian publishers leave it out)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>13</td>\n<td>pronoun–antecedent agreement</td>\n<td><em>Each of the emails has been sent to its recipients.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>14</td>\n<td>a misplaced modifier</td>\n<td><em>the paper clips that were left over to the department</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>An <strong>antecedent</strong> is the noun that a pronoun refers back to; a <strong>modifier</strong> must stand next to what it describes.</p>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w12/editing\">editing</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w12/proofreading\">proofreading</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w12/the-fourteen-most-common-surface-errors\">the 14 errors</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"writing-for-an-exam\">Writing for an exam</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Keep it simple</strong>: “Simple done accurately is better than complicated done inaccurately” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SP97dPNQmLM&amp;t=40s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:40</a>).</li>\n<li>Prepare <strong>set phrases</strong> (not rehearsed answers) and <strong>linking words</strong> for each job: compare (<em>in the same way</em>), contrast (<em>however</em>), give an example (<em>for instance</em>), give your opinion (<em>from my point of view</em>), generalise (<em>overall</em>), express certainty (<em>undoubtedly</em>), partial agreement (<em>to some extent</em>), cause (<em>owing to</em>), effect (<em>consequently</em>), time (<em>to begin with</em>), addition (<em>moreover</em>), condition (<em>provided that</em>), conclusion (<em>in short</em>).</li>\n<li>Use <strong>no contractions</strong>, and fewer words: drop <strong>deadweight</strong> words (<em>very, so, really, a lot</em>); use <strong>strong</strong> words (<em>elated</em> for <em>very, very happy</em>; <em>terrified</em> for <em>so scared</em>); avoid <em>there is</em> and <em>there are</em>: <em>We may encounter many problems</em> (5 words) beats <em>There are many problems that we may encounter</em> (8 words).</li>\n<li>Read the kinds of writing the exam asks for; “write less and check more”; get your practice work checked by someone who writes better.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w12/writing-for-an-exam\">writing for an exam</a>, <a href=\"#/english1/w12/set-phrases-and-linking-words\">linking words</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Common mistakes in Week 12</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>A topic sentence that is a plain fact, or one hidden in the middle of the paragraph.</li>\n<li>A stand-alone paragraph with no concluding sentence.</li>\n<li>Comparing things whose differences are obvious.</li>\n<li><em>I think, you, can’t, awesome, last but not least</em> in a formal report.</li>\n<li>Proofreading for everything at once, and skipping your own list of errors.</li>\n<li><em>your</em> for <em>you’re</em>; <em>advise</em> for <em>advice</em>; ✗ <em>Each of the emails have been sent to their recipients</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>Full revision:</strong> <a href=\"#/english1/w12\">Week 12</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"how-it-all-fits-together\">How it all fits together</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>English 1 climbs a ladder: <strong>sounds</strong> (Week 1) → <strong>syllables and stress</strong> (Week 8) → <strong>words</strong> and their classes (Weeks 2 and 3) → the <strong>verb</strong> and agreement (Week 5) → <strong>sentences and paragraphs</strong> (Weeks 10 to 12) → whole <strong>talks and texts</strong> (Weeks 4, 9 and 11). Beside the ladder run the four skills of Lecture 1.2: listening (Week 7), speaking (Weeks 4 and 9), reading (Week 6) and writing (Weeks 10 to 12).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The threads that run through the course:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Thread</th>\n<th>Where it comes back</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Sounds, not letters</td>\n<td>26 letters but 44 sounds (1.3); <em>a</em> or <em>an</em> by the next sound (2.5); the plural /s/, /z/, /iz/ by the last sound (8.2); syllables counted by vowel sounds (8.1, 8.5)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Aspiration and voicing</td>\n<td>the two palm tests (1.5); voiceless p and k, voiced b and g (1.7); /s/ and /z/ in plurals (8.2); the puff on p, t, k in stressed syllables (8.3)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stress</td>\n<td>words are stressed differently (4.1); the stress patterns (8.1, 8.4); stress decides the puff (8.3); 13 or 30 (11.3; the stress tip thir-TEEN or THIR-ty is ours)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Agreement</td>\n<td>only one verb agrees with the subject (2.1); person and number (5.4, 5.5); the head of the noun phrase (10.2); errors 11 and 13 of proofreading (12.4)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Modality</td>\n<td>the modal verbs (3.4); <em>will</em> makes the future (5.3); modality leaves room for disagreement (12.3)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Clauses and linking words</td>\n<td>coordinating and subordinating conjunctions (2.4); summary words in reading (6.1); topic sentences, transitional devices (12.2); one verb a sentence (11.8); commas and comma splices (12.4); linking words in exams (12.5)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Something to say</td>\n<td>content before accent (4.1, 4.4); content first in writing (10.3); content and planning in essays (11.7)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Slow and simple</td>\n<td>speak slowly (4.2, 9.2); a steady pace in tests (9.6); small words and simple sentences (10.3, 11.8, 12.5)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Facts, not feelings</td>\n<td>fact or opinion (6.1); quantify (4.4); facts, not attitudes (10.5, 10.6); facts in business letters (11.2); the objective tone (12.3)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Drafts and revision</td>\n<td>edit with CED (9.1); several drafts (10.3 to 11.8); editing and proofreading (12.4)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dictionary and thesaurus</td>\n<td>3.2, 4.1, 4.4, 8.4 (stress marks), 11.7, 11.8</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Listening and prediction</td>\n<td>active listening (7.1) in a GD (9.3); predictive listening (7.4) to predict blanks in tests (7.5)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Timed tests</td>\n<td>reading (6.6), listening (7.5), speaking (9.6), writing (12.5): read the task first, budget your time, never get stuck, check at the end</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h3 id=\"which-tense-when\">Which tense when</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>You want to say …</th>\n<th>Use</th>\n<th>The lectures’ example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>a finished past event</td>\n<td>simple past</td>\n<td><em>She returned from the conference yesterday.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the earlier of two past events</td>\n<td>past perfect</td>\n<td><em>They had reached home before the parcel arrived.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>an action in progress in the past</td>\n<td>past progressive</td>\n<td><em>She was walking back to her lab.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>an earlier past action that went on for a while</td>\n<td>past perfect progressive</td>\n<td><em>She had been knitting the quilt … before the family arrived.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a state, a habit, or what happens as you speak</td>\n<td>simple present</td>\n<td><em>He reads the newspaper in the morning.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a past action that matters now</td>\n<td>present perfect</td>\n<td><em>The train has finally arrived.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>an action in progress now</td>\n<td>present progressive</td>\n<td><em>She is eating a mango.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>an action from the past up to now</td>\n<td>present perfect progressive</td>\n<td><em>The baby has been crying for long.</em> (Standard: <em>for a long time</em>.)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a prediction</td>\n<td>simple future</td>\n<td><em>It will rain in the month of June.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>something finished before a future time</td>\n<td>future perfect</td>\n<td><em>She will have completed her work by then.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a timetabled future, a routine, a habit</td>\n<td>future progressive</td>\n<td><em>I’ll be taking my day off on Saturdays.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a future state that will have lasted a long time</td>\n<td>future perfect progressive</td>\n<td><em>I will have been living in Chennai for over ten years.</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h3 id=\"which-method-when\">Which method when</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Your task</th>\n<th>Method</th>\n<th>Week</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>choose an article</td>\n<td><em>a</em> or <em>an</em> by the next sound; <em>the</em> when the listener knows which one; no article for routine places and general mass nouns</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>choose a modal</td>\n<td>ability <em>can</em>; a polite request <em>could</em>; formal permission <em>may</em>; advice <em>should</em>; obligation <em>must</em> or <em>have to</em>; a ban <em>mustn’t</em>; no need <em>don’t have to</em></td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>get the gist of a text</td>\n<td>skim</td>\n<td>6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>find one fact</td>\n<td>scan</td>\n<td>6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>study a chapter</td>\n<td>SQ3R or PQRST</td>\n<td>6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>follow a long talk</td>\n<td>before: get alert, read up; during: key concepts, keywords, doubts; after: review</td>\n<td>7</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>argue a debatable point</td>\n<td>PREP</td>\n<td>9</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>inform and predict</td>\n<td>past–present–future</td>\n<td>9</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>recommend a business or policy action</td>\n<td>problem–cause–solution</td>\n<td>9</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>persuade</td>\n<td>AIDA</td>\n<td>9</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>give directions</td>\n<td>numbered steps, in order, with names and numbers</td>\n<td>11</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>report on a chart</td>\n<td>what, how, trends, details, differences, conclusion</td>\n<td>11</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>link sentences</td>\n<td>add <em>moreover</em>; contrast <em>however</em>; cause <em>owing to</em>; effect <em>consequently</em>; conclude <em>in conclusion</em></td>\n<td>12</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>finish a draft</td>\n<td>edit (content, structure, clarity, style), then proofread (one error at a time)</td>\n<td>12</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2 id=\"check-yourself\">Check yourself</h2>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Sounds before letters (Weeks 1, 2 and 8). (a) <em>A</em> or <em>an</em>: ___ hour, ___ European, ___ MP. (b) How does the plural ending of <em>judges</em>, <em>books</em> and <em>trees</em> sound? (c) How many syllables are in <em>judges</em> and in <em>boys</em>?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>(a) Go by the first <strong>sound</strong> of the next word.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>hour</em>: the h is silent, so the first sound is a vowel → <strong>an hour</strong>.</li>\n<li><em>European</em>: the first sound is /j/, the y of <em>you</em>, a consonant → <strong>a European</strong>.</li>\n<li><em>MP</em>: we say “em”, which starts with a vowel sound → <strong>an MP</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>(b) Go by the last <strong>sound</strong> of the singular.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>judge</em> ends in /dʒ/ → a short vowel comes in → <strong>/iz/</strong>.</li>\n<li><em>book</em> ends in /k/ → <strong>/s/</strong>.</li>\n<li><em>tree</em> ends in a vowel → <strong>/z/</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>(c) Count the vowel <strong>sounds</strong>.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>judges</em> = judg-es: two vowel sounds → <strong>2 syllables</strong>. The /iz/ adds a syllable.</li>\n<li><em>boys</em> has one diphthong, /ɔɪ/ → <strong>1 syllable</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Every answer depends on sounds, not letters: the lesson of Week 1.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Stress and aspiration (Weeks 1 and 8). Mark the stress, and say whether the p, t or k sound gets a puff of air: (a) <em>There was a decrease in sales.</em> (b) <em>Sales decrease in winter.</em> (c) <em>potato</em> (d) <em>spring</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>(a) <em>decrease</em> is a noun here. Two-syllable nouns stress the first syllable: <strong>DE</strong>-crease. The k sound (spelled c) starts the unstressed syllable → <strong>no puff</strong>.</p>\n<p>(b) Here it is a verb. Two-syllable verbs stress the second syllable: de-<strong>CREASE</strong>. The k sound starts the stressed syllable → <strong>a puff</strong>, [kʰ].</p>\n<p>(c) po-<strong>TA</strong>-to: the t at the start of the stressed <em>TA</em> gets the puff → [tʰ] (Lecture 8.3).</p>\n<p>(d) <em>spring</em> has one syllable, and its p follows s → <strong>no puff</strong>. The word also fits Week 1’s three-consonant template: s + p + r.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Find the subject, then choose the verb (Weeks 5 and 10). (a) <em>The history teachers of our school (has / have) set the paper.</em> (b) <em>Either my siblings or my father (is / are) with me at home.</em> (c) <em>Five years (is / are) not a long time in history.</em> (d) Name the tense form of the verb in (a). (Sentence (a) is mine; (b) and (c) are the lectures’.)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>(a) In the noun + noun group <em>history teachers</em>, the second noun is the head; <em>of our school</em> is a modifier. <em>Teachers</em> is plural → <strong>have</strong>.</p>\n<p>(b) A plural and a singular joined by <em>or</em>: the part nearer the verb decides. That is <em>my father</em>, singular → <strong>is</strong>.</p>\n<p>(c) An amount of time is taken as a whole, so it is singular → <strong>is</strong>.</p>\n<p>(d) <em>have set</em> = <em>have</em> + V3, so it is the <strong>present perfect</strong>. <em>Have</em> carries the present tense and agrees with the plural head <em>teachers</em>.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Modals and modality (Weeks 3 and 12). (a) What is the difference between <em>You mustn’t smoke in here</em> and <em>You don’t have to come</em>? (b) Rewrite for a formal report: <em>I think this policy will never hurt anybody.</em> (Sentence (b) is mine.)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>(a)</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>mustn’t</em> = an obligation <strong>not</strong> to do it: smoking is not allowed. It is like <em>can’t</em>.</li>\n<li><em>don’t have to</em> = <strong>no</strong> obligation: come or not, as you like. It is like <em>needn’t</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>(b)</p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>I think</em> puts a person first; formal writing puts the idea first (objective tone, rule 1).</li>\n<li><em>will never hurt anybody</em> is absolute: no writer can be sure of that.</li>\n<li>A modality of possibility leaves the reader room to disagree (rule 4).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>There is a possibility that the policy works well for everyone</em>, the lecture’s own model.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Polite and clear (Week 4). Make this request polite, then mark the pauses with # (short) and ## (long): <em>Tell me the way to the library the canteen and the hostel office.</em> (The sentence is mine.)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li>A bare command sounds rude in English, so turn it into a <strong>question</strong> and add polite words: <em>Excuse me, sir, will you please tell me the way to the library, the canteen and the hostel office?</em></li>\n<li>Pause after the polite opener <em>Excuse me</em> and after the word of address <em>sir</em>.</li>\n<li>Pause after each item in the series.</li>\n<li>Use the long pause at the end of the sentence.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>Excuse me # sir # will you please tell me the way to the library # the canteen # and the hostel office ##</em></p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Judge the reasoning (Weeks 6 and 12). Name each one: (a) <em>Our college is the best because no other college is better.</em> (b) <em>The new canteen opened in June and the exam results fell in July, so the canteen caused the fall.</em> (c) A party’s poster shows its candidate beside the national flag. (d) <em>Delhi is the capital of India.</em> (e) <em>Delhi has the best food in India.</em> (All are mine.)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li>(a) <strong>Circular reasoning</strong>: “no other college is better” only repeats “it is the best”. No real reason is given.</li>\n<li>(b) <strong>False cause</strong>: the fall came <em>after</em> the canteen opened, but coming after is not being caused by.</li>\n<li>(c) <strong>Transfer</strong>, a propaganda technique: the love people feel for the flag is meant to pass to the candidate.</li>\n<li>(d) A <strong>fact</strong>: anyone can check it with objective evidence.</li>\n<li>(e) An <strong>opinion</strong>: <em>best</em> is a judgement, and fair people can disagree.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A professional writer (Week 12) makes no judgement like (e) without evidence, and cites authentic sources for facts like (d).</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">A group discussion goes wrong (Weeks 7 and 9). A member begins her point. Within seconds you think, “She knows nothing about this”, plan your reply while she talks, and cut in: “No, that is completely wrong.” Name the listening barriers, then say what you should do instead. (The situation is mine.)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Judging the speaker too soon</strong>: you decided before she had finished.</li>\n<li><strong>Interruption</strong> by you, and a reply planned while you should have been listening.</li>\n<li>Probably <strong>defensive communication</strong>, if you felt you had to prove yourself right.</li>\n<li>Instead, listen actively, note her point, and wait for your turn: each member has only about a minute.</li>\n<li>Then disagree politely, with a reason: <em>I see where you’re coming from and I agree, but …</em> or <em>I beg to differ, but …</em>. Do not dominate: “this is a discussion, not a debate”.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Plan a 10-minute talk to persuade first-year students to join the college reading club (Weeks 4 and 9). (a) Which scripting strategy? (b) About how many words? (c) How do you open and close? (d) Name two delivery habits from Week 4. (The talk is mine.)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>(a) <strong>AIDA</strong>, because the aim is to persuade: Attention (a surprising fact or a question), Interest (what the club offers), Desire (help them picture themselves enjoying it), Action (join today).</p>\n<p>(b) The lecture’s guide is 1,200 words for 10 minutes: 1,200 ÷ 10 = 120 words a minute. So about <strong>1,200 words</strong>.</p>\n<p>(c) Open with a rhetorical question, such as <em>When did you last read a book just for fun?</em> Close with a summary and a <strong>call for action</strong>: <em>Fill in the form on your way out.</em></p>\n<p>(d) Any two: speak <strong>slowly</strong>, pausing after each phrase and clause; keep <strong>eye contact</strong>, panning slowly across the room; greet the audience with a smile; dress for the occasion.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Find every problem, then rewrite for a formal complaint (Weeks 10 to 12): <em>“In my personal opinion the hostel water problem is just the tip of the iceberg, there are a lot of students who are really, really angry and the warden don’t care.”</em> (The sentence is mine.)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li><em>In my personal opinion</em>: wordy, and in the first person. Cut it.</li>\n<li><em>the tip of the iceberg</em>: jargon, a worn-out picture. Say what you mean.</li>\n<li><em>… iceberg, there are …</em>: a <strong>comma splice</strong>, two sentences joined by a comma (error 6).</li>\n<li><em>there are a lot of students who</em>: <em>there are</em> and the deadweight <em>a lot of</em>. Use <em>many students</em>.</li>\n<li><em>really, really angry</em>: emotional and empty. Give a fact instead.</li>\n<li><em>the warden don’t</em>: subject–verb agreement (error 11). <em>Warden</em> is singular, so <em>does not</em>; and <em>don’t</em> is a contraction.</li>\n<li><em>don’t care</em>: an attitude, not a fact. Say what the warden has or has not done.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>A formal version:</strong> <em>The hostel has a serious water problem. Many students have complained. The warden has not yet acted.</em> Three short sentences, one verb each, only facts. In a real letter, add the numbers: how many days, how many complaints.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Turn a table into words (Weeks 6 and 11). A survey asked 200 students how they come to college: bus 90, walk 50, cycle 40, car 20. (a) Check the total and turn each number into a chunk. (b) Write the report’s key sentences. (The numbers are mine.)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>(a)</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Total: 90 + 50 + 40 + 20 = 200. It adds up.</li>\n<li>Bus: 90 ÷ 200 = 0.45 = 45% → <strong>nearly half</strong>.</li>\n<li>Walk: 50 ÷ 200 = 0.25 = 25% → <strong>one fourth</strong>.</li>\n<li>Cycle: 40 ÷ 200 = 0.2 = 20% → <strong>one fifth</strong>.</li>\n<li>Car: 20 ÷ 200 = 0.1 = 10% → <strong>one in ten</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>(b) In the order of Lecture 11.5:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>What and how: <em>The table shows how 200 students travel to college. It gives four ways of travel, with the number of students for each.</em></li>\n<li>The main trend and the details: <em>Nearly half come by bus. One fourth walk, one fifth cycle, and only one in ten comes by car.</em></li>\n<li>What it does not tell: <em>The table gives no date or place for the survey.</em></li>\n<li>A concluding remark: <em>Most students come by bus or on foot</em>, since 90 + 50 = 140, and 140 ÷ 200 = 0.7 = 70%.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In a reading test (Week 6), “nearly half” is the kind of <strong>paraphrase</strong> of “45%” that a correct option uses.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Label this paragraph (Weeks 2 and 12): <em>“Printed books still help students study better than phones. A printed page does not tire the eyes as quickly as a bright screen. Moreover, a book brings no messages or alerts to break your attention. For instance, a student can read a textbook for an hour without a single interruption. In short, for serious study, paper is still the better choice.”</em> Which sentence does which job, how is the paragraph unified, and does it need its last sentence? (The paragraph is mine.)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li>Sentence 1 is the <strong>topic sentence</strong>: an arguable claim, placed first, as professional writing recommends.</li>\n<li>Sentences 2 to 4 are <strong>supporting sentences</strong>: a reason, a second reason, and an example.</li>\n<li>Sentence 5 is the <strong>concluding sentence</strong>: it sums up and recommends. The paragraph stands alone, so the concluding sentence is <strong>required</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Unity</strong>: the whole paragraph keeps to one idea, that paper is better for study; the key words <em>printed</em>, <em>book</em> and <em>study</em> repeat.</li>\n<li><strong>Transitional devices</strong>: <em>Moreover</em> adds, <em>For instance</em> brings an example, <em>In short</em> concludes. Like the conjunctions of Week 2 they link ideas, here from one sentence to the next.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Count and cut (Weeks 11 and 12): <em>“There are many problems that we may encounter due to the fact that the road is waterlogged.”</em> Shorten it step by step, and count the words at each step. (The sentence is mine, built from the lectures’ phrases.)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li>Count: There, are, many, problems, that, we, may, encounter, due, to, the, fact, that, the, road, is, waterlogged → <strong>17 words</strong>.</li>\n<li>Avoid <em>there are … that</em> (12.5): <em>We may encounter many problems</em>.</li>\n<li><em>Due to the fact that</em> is an unnecessary phrase (12.4): use <em>because</em>. Now: <em>We may encounter many problems because the road is waterlogged</em> → <strong>10 words</strong>.</li>\n<li>Lecture 11.8 allows 10 words, but only <strong>one verb</strong> a sentence, and this one has two (<em>may encounter</em>, <em>is</em>). Split it, and use a shorter word for <em>encounter</em> (three syllables): <em>The road is waterlogged. We may face many problems.</em> → 4 + 5 = <strong>9 words</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>The road is waterlogged. We may face many problems.</em> That saves 17 − 9 = 8 words, and the meaning is the same.</p>\n</div></details>\n"});
